window.QBANK={"1":[{"i":"REG-I-A-0001","t":"Which of the following persons does NOT have unlimited authority to represent a taxpayer before every function of the IRS, including Appeals and Collection?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following persons does NOT have unlimited authority to represent a taxpayer before every function of the IRS, including Appeals and Collection?","d":"","o":["A paid preparer whose only credential is a PTIN and an Annual Filing Season Program Record of Completion","An enrolled agent who has passed all three parts of the Special Enrollment Examination and holds a current enrollment card","A CPA in good standing with the licensing authority of a state","An attorney in good standing before the highest court of a state"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.3; C230 §10.7; Pub 947; Rev. Proc. 2014-42","rat":"Holders of that record have only limited representation rights, and only for returns they prepared and signed.","ex":["Holders of that record have only limited representation rights, and only for returns they prepared and signed. Unlimited rights belong to attorneys, CPAs and enrolled agents; enrolled actuaries and enrolled retirement plan agents may practice only on the specific matters listed in Circular 230 section 10.3(d)(2) and (e)(2).","Enrolled agents are enrolled to practice and have unlimited representation rights before all IRS offices.","A CPA in good standing may practice before the IRS without further enrollment and has unlimited rights.","An attorney in good standing has unlimited practice rights; no IRS enrollment is required."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Only attorneys, CPAs and enrolled agents have unlimited IRS practice rights; enrolled actuaries and retirement plan agents may practice only on the matters listed in Circular 230; a Record of Completion gives limited rights only.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0002","t":"Mira Kowalski holds a PTIN but no CPA, attorney or enrolled agent credential and no Annual Filing Season Program Record of Completion. In 2026 she prepared and signed a client's 2025 Form 1040. The IRS now opens an examination of that return. May Mira represent the client before the revenue agent?","mc":{"s":"Mira Kowalski holds a PTIN but no CPA, attorney or enrolled agent credential and no Annual Filing Season Program Record of Completion. In 2026 she prepared and signed a client's 2025 Form 1040. The IRS now opens an examination of that return. May Mira represent the client before the revenue agent?","d":"","o":["Yes, but only before revenue agents, since she prepared and signed the return","Yes, in any IRS matter, if the client signs a power of attorney naming her","Yes, because she is compensated to prepare the return and is therefore a tax return preparer","No, because she has no credential or Record of Completion giving representation rights"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.3; C230 §10.7; Pub 947","rat":"For returns prepared and signed after 2015, an unenrolled preparer without a credential or Record of Completion has no right to represent the taxpayer in an examination.","ex":["This was the rule for returns prepared and signed before 2016. It no longer applies to a preparer with no credential and no Record of Completion.","A power of attorney only names a representative; it does not give authority to a person who is not eligible to practice.","Being a paid preparer creates preparer duties and penalties. It does not give the right to represent a taxpayer.","For returns prepared and signed after 2015, an unenrolled preparer without a credential or Record of Completion has no right to represent the taxpayer in an examination."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A preparer with no credential and no Record of Completion cannot represent a client in an examination of a return prepared after 2015.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0003","t":"A client signs Form 8821, Tax Information Authorization, naming her CPA. What may the CPA do under that form?","mc":{"s":"A client signs Form 8821, Tax Information Authorization, naming her CPA. What may the CPA do under that form?","d":"","o":["Receive the client's tax information, but not represent the client or sign for her","Represent the client at a Collection Appeals hearing and sign a closing agreement for her","Sign the client's amended return and endorse the resulting refund check","Tell the IRS what settlement amount the client would accept in an examination"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.6; Pub 947; Form 8821 instructions","rat":"Form 8821 authorizes access to tax information only.","ex":["Form 8821 authorizes access to tax information only. Representation before the IRS requires a power of attorney on Form 2848.","Representing a taxpayer and signing agreements require Form 2848, not Form 8821.","A preparer signs only as preparer. Endorsing or negotiating a client's refund check is prohibited by Circular 230.","Negotiating a settlement is representation, which requires Form 2848."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Form 8821 authorizes receipt of tax information only; representation requires Form 2848 power of attorney.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0004","t":"Circular 230 lets certain persons represent others before the IRS without being an attorney, CPA or enrolled agent. In which situation is the representative NOT covered by such an exception?","mc":{"s":"Circular 230 lets certain persons represent others before the IRS without being an attorney, CPA or enrolled agent. In which situation is the representative NOT covered by such an exception?","d":"","o":["A full-time employee represents his employer in an IRS matter","A general partner represents her partnership before the IRS","A taxpayer's adult child represents the taxpayer, her parent, at the IRS","An unpaid friend with no credential and no family tie represents the taxpayer"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.7(c); Pub 947","rat":"Friendship is not a listed relationship.","ex":["A regular full-time employee may represent his employer, so this is permitted.","A general partner may represent the partnership; this is a listed exception.","An individual may represent a member of his or her immediate family without enrolling.","Friendship is not a listed relationship. The exceptions cover the taxpayer herself and specified relationships such as family, corporate officers, partners and fiduciaries."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Circular 230 exceptions cover the taxpayer, family, employees, partners, officers and fiduciaries, not an unrelated friend.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0005","t":"During work on a client's 2025 return, Hana Cho, CPA, learns that the client omitted $30,000 of income from the 2023 return Hana's firm prepared. What must Hana do under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"During work on a client's 2025 return, Hana Cho, CPA, learns that the client omitted $30,000 of income from the 2023 return Hana's firm prepared. What must Hana do under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["Promptly advise the client of the omission and its consequences","Report the omission to the IRS within 30 days","Amend the 2023 return without informing the client because the firm prepared it","Resign from the engagement and notify the client's lender of the reason"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.21; IRC §7216","rat":"A practitioner who knows of a client's error or omission must advise the client of it and its consequences.","ex":["A practitioner who knows of a client's error or omission must advise the client of it and its consequences. Circular 230 does not require reporting it to the IRS.","Circular 230 does not require the practitioner to disclose the error to the IRS, and return information generally cannot be disclosed without consent.","A practitioner cannot amend a return without the client's knowledge and signature.","Resigning is not required, and telling a third party would breach confidentiality."],"wt":["","limit","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner who knows of a client's return error must advise the client of the error and consequences, but need not report it to the IRS.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0006","t":"Rafael Ortiz, CPA, receives a proper written IRS request for records he holds for a client's 2024 return, which is under examination. The records are not privileged, and the client tells Rafael to ignore the request. What must Rafael do?","mc":{"s":"Rafael Ortiz, CPA, receives a proper written IRS request for records he holds for a client's 2024 return, which is under examination. The records are not privileged, and the client tells Rafael to ignore the request. What must Rafael do?","d":"","o":["Refuse, because client confidentiality overrides any IRS request for records unless the IRS obtains a summons","Wait until the IRS issues a summons before providing anything","Give the IRS only the records that are favorable to the client","Promptly submit the records, because he has no good-faith basis to claim privilege"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.20(a)","rat":"A practitioner must promptly give the IRS records it properly requests unless he reasonably and in good faith believes they are privileged.","ex":["There is no general confidentiality exception to a proper IRS request. Only a good-faith belief that the records are privileged permits refusal.","A practitioner must not delay a proper informal request by insisting on a summons when no privilege applies.","Selective production would breach the duty to respond promptly and could itself be sanctionable conduct.","A practitioner must promptly give the IRS records it properly requests unless he reasonably and in good faith believes they are privileged. The client's instruction is not a basis to refuse."],"wt":["trap","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner must promptly submit properly requested IRS records unless in good faith believing them privileged; client instruction is no excuse.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0007","t":"Delia Nunez, EA, is asked to accept one of four fee arrangements by a client. Which arrangement is permitted under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Delia Nunez, EA, is asked to accept one of four fee arrangements by a client. Which arrangement is permitted under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["25% of any tax reduction, for representing the client in an examination of a filed 2023 return","15% of the refund, for preparing the client's original 2025 return","A fee that the client sets after the work, payable only if the client is satisfied","10% of the tax saved, for advising the client on a proposed position before any return is filed"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.27","rat":"A contingent fee is permitted for services connected with an IRS examination of, or challenge to, an original return.","ex":["A contingent fee is permitted for services connected with an IRS examination of, or challenge to, an original return.","A contingent fee for preparing an original return is prohibited.","Outside the permitted categories, a fee may not depend on the outcome of the matter.","Advice before a return is filed is not an examination or judicial matter, so a contingent fee is not permitted."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Circular 230 permits contingent fees only for IRS examinations, challenges to original returns and certain refund claims or court matters, not original return preparation.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0008","t":"Lucas Ferreira, CPA, considers four fee proposals. Which one is permitted under both Circular 230 and the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?","mc":{"s":"Lucas Ferreira, CPA, considers four fee proposals. Which one is permitted under both Circular 230 and the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?","d":"","o":["25% of the refund, for preparing an original return for a non-attest client","20% of the refund, for preparing an amended return for a non-attest client","10% of the tax benefit, for tax planning advice to a client for whom Lucas performs the audit","30% of the tax saved, for representing a non-attest client in an IRS examination"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.27; AICPA Code 1.510.001","rat":"Both sets of rules allow a contingent fee for representing a client in an examination by a taxing authority.","ex":["Both sets of rules prohibit a contingent fee for preparing an original return.","The AICPA Code prohibits a contingent fee for preparing an amended return, apart from the examination and court exceptions.","The AICPA Code bars any contingent fee from an attest client, so this fails.","Both sets of rules allow a contingent fee for representing a client in an examination by a taxing authority."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Contingent fees for representation in an IRS examination are allowed under both Circular 230 and the AICPA Code for non-attest clients.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0009","t":"Nora Patel, CPA, prepared a client's 2025 return. The client disputes Nora's $2,000 fee and has not paid it. The client asks for the records she supplied, which the client needs to file the client's federal return. Nora is holding them until she is paid. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Nora Patel, CPA, prepared a client's 2025 return. The client disputes Nora's $2,000 fee and has not paid it. The client asks for the records she supplied, which the client needs to file the client's federal return. Nora is holding them until she is paid. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Nora must return the records promptly despite the fee dispute; she may keep copies","Nora may keep the records until the fee is paid in full, because she holds a lien","Nora must return the records only after the client files a formal request with the IRS","Nora must return the records but may first charge a $500 release fee"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.28; AICPA Code 1.400.200; C230 §10.28(b)","rat":"A practitioner must return client records on request, and a fee dispute does not excuse holding them when the client needs them to meet tax obligations.","ex":["A practitioner must return client records on request, and a fee dispute does not excuse holding them when the client needs them to meet tax obligations.","Circular 230 requires return of the records the client needs to comply with federal tax obligations even when a fee dispute exists; a state-law lien does not let the practitioner hold them as leverage (the Section 10.28(b) exception covers only records already attached to a return or state-law lien rights over other records).","The client's own request is enough; the IRS is not involved.","A practitioner may not condition the return of records on payment of a release fee."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner must promptly return client records the client needs for tax compliance despite a fee dispute, and may keep copies.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0010","t":"Priya Shah's CPA firm represents Kim Yoon in an IRS examination. The firm also represents Kim's former partner Lee Grant, whose examination requires Lee to take a position directly adverse to Kim. What is required for the firm to continue with both clients?","mc":{"s":"Priya Shah's CPA firm represents Kim Yoon in an IRS examination. The firm also represents Kim's former partner Lee Grant, whose examination requires Lee to take a position directly adverse to Kim. What is required for the firm to continue with both clients?","d":"","o":["Only Kim's oral consent, given at the start of the engagement, since Kim is the earlier client and Lee's matter arose later","No consent is needed, because both matters are before the same IRS office and involve the same tax years","Each client gives informed written consent within 30 days, and the firm reasonably believes it can serve both","Written consent from the IRS examiner assigned to both cases"],"a":3,"cite":"C230 §10.29","rat":"A conflict may be waived only if the practitioner reasonably believes he can provide competent, diligent representation and each affected client gives informed written consent within 30 days of learning of the conflict.","ex":["Consent must come from every affected client, and it must be in writing.","Representing directly adverse interests creates a conflict regardless of the office.","A conflict may be waived only if the practitioner reasonably believes he can provide competent, diligent representation and each affected client gives informed written consent within 30 days of learning of the conflict.","The waiver comes from the clients, not from the IRS."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Directly adverse client conflicts are waivable only if the practitioner reasonably believes he can serve both and each client gives informed written consent within 30 days.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0011","t":"Which of the following is prohibited by Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is prohibited by Circular 230?","d":"","o":["A practitioner asks that a client's refund be deposited directly into the client's own bank account","A practitioner accepts a client's check payable to the firm for tax preparation fees","A practitioner returns a client's refund check to the client by certified mail","A practitioner endorses a client's Treasury refund check and deposits it in the firm's account"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.31","rat":"A practitioner may not endorse or negotiate a check issued to a client by the government for a federal tax liability.","ex":["Direct deposit to the client's own account is proper; the practitioner does not handle the funds.","The prohibition covers government checks issued to the client, not payments the client makes for fees.","Delivering the check to the client is not endorsing or negotiating it.","A practitioner may not endorse or negotiate a check issued to a client by the government for a federal tax liability."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner may not endorse or negotiate a Treasury refund check issued to a client.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0012","t":"Jonas Weiss prepares the Schedule C for a client. The client provides $45,000 of meals expenses on gross receipts of $60,000 and offers no explanation. Under Circular 230, what is Jonas's duty?","mc":{"s":"Jonas Weiss prepares the Schedule C for a client. The client provides $45,000 of meals expenses on gross receipts of $60,000 and offers no explanation. Under Circular 230, what is Jonas's duty?","d":"","o":["He may rely on the information in good faith, but must inquire because it looks incomplete","He must audit all of the client's records before preparing the return","He may accept the figures as given, since the client is responsible for the return's contents","He must refuse the engagement and report the client to the IRS"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.34(d); C230 §10.22","rat":"A practitioner may rely without independent verification but may not ignore the implications of information he knows, and must inquire when it looks incorrect, inconsistent or incomplete.","ex":["A practitioner may rely without independent verification but may not ignore the implications of information he knows, and must inquire when it looks incorrect, inconsistent or incomplete.","There is no duty to audit or verify client information in every case.","Good-faith reliance is not available when the information is inconsistent on its face; inquiry is required.","Nothing requires refusal or reporting; the required step is to inquire."],"wt":["","trap","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner may rely in good faith on client information but must inquire when it appears incorrect, inconsistent or incomplete.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0013","t":"Owen Blake, CPA, is preparing a return that includes a deduction with a reasonable basis but without substantial authority. No reportable transaction is involved and Owen is not acting willfully. Which action satisfies Circular 230 as to that position?","mc":{"s":"Owen Blake, CPA, is preparing a return that includes a deduction with a reasonable basis but without substantial authority. No reportable transaction is involved and Owen is not acting willfully. Which action satisfies Circular 230 as to that position?","d":"","o":["Sign the return after the position is adequately disclosed on it","Sign the return without disclosure, since reasonable basis alone is enough","Sign the return without disclosure and tell the client to expect a penalty","Sign the return if the client agrees to pay any resulting penalty"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.34(a); IRC §6694(a)(2); Reg §1.6694-2(c)","rat":"A position that lacks substantial authority but has a reasonable basis may be signed if it is adequately disclosed.","ex":["A position that lacks substantial authority but has a reasonable basis may be signed if it is adequately disclosed.","Without disclosure the position is unreasonable because it lacks substantial authority.","Informing the client does not cure a position that is unreasonable when undisclosed.","A client's promise to pay is not a substitute for the disclosure or authority required."],"wt":["","concept","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A position with reasonable basis but without substantial authority may be signed only if adequately disclosed on the return.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0014","t":"Kai Nakamura, CPA, advises a client about an aggressive return position that could draw a penalty. Under Circular 230, what must Kai tell the client?","mc":{"s":"Kai Nakamura, CPA, advises a client about an aggressive return position that could draw a penalty. Under Circular 230, what must Kai tell the client?","d":"","o":["Nothing, if the client has signed an engagement letter accepting the risk","The penalties that may apply and how disclosure can avoid them","Only the interest that would accrue, because penalties are the IRS's concern","That the position may not be taken unless the client obtains a written tax opinion"],"a":2,"cite":"C230 §10.34(c)","rat":"A practitioner must advise a client of potentially applicable penalties and of the opportunity to avoid them through disclosure, and explain the disclosure requirements.","ex":["An engagement letter does not remove the duty to inform.","A practitioner must advise a client of potentially applicable penalties and of the opportunity to avoid them through disclosure, and explain the disclosure requirements.","The duty specifically covers penalties, not just interest.","Circular 230 does not require an opinion for every aggressive position."],"wt":["concept","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner must advise a client of potential penalties on a position and how adequate disclosure can avoid them.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0015","t":"Which of the following is prohibited when a practitioner gives written tax advice under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is prohibited when a practitioner gives written tax advice under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["Making a reasonable factual assumption and stating it in the advice","Relying on a client representation that the practitioner has reason to believe is reasonable","Assuming the return will not be audited or the issue will not be raised","Considering all relevant facts that the practitioner knows"],"a":3,"cite":"C230 §10.37","rat":"A practitioner may not take into account the possibility that a return will not be audited or that an issue will not be raised.","ex":["Reasonable assumptions may be used if they are identified and supported.","Reasonable reliance on representations is allowed.","A practitioner may not take into account the possibility that a return will not be audited or that an issue will not be raised.","Considering all relevant facts is a requirement, not a prohibition."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Written tax advice may not take into account the possibility that a return will not be audited or an issue not raised.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0016","t":"Elena Rossi is the managing partner of a CPA firm. She knows that several staff members routinely sign returns with unsupported positions. The firm has no review procedures and Elena has taken no steps to change that. What is Elena's exposure under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Elena Rossi is the managing partner of a CPA firm. She knows that several staff members routinely sign returns with unsupported positions. The firm has no review procedures and Elena has taken no steps to change that. What is Elena's exposure under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["None, because only the staff members who signed the returns are responsible","None, unless the IRS first assesses a penalty against a client","Only a civil penalty under the return preparer rules, since Circular 230 discipline applies to the individual signers","She may be sanctioned for not taking reasonable steps to ensure firm-wide compliance procedures"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.36","rat":"A practitioner with principal authority over a firm's practice must take reasonable steps to ensure adequate compliance procedures, and may be sanctioned when the firm shows a pattern of noncompliance.","ex":["Circular 230 places responsibility on those with principal authority as well.","Sanctions do not depend on a client penalty.","Both regimes can apply; the firm-level duty is set by Circular 230.","A practitioner with principal authority over a firm's practice must take reasonable steps to ensure adequate compliance procedures, and may be sanctioned when the firm shows a pattern of noncompliance."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner with principal authority over a firm must take reasonable steps to ensure firm-wide compliance procedures or face Circular 230 sanction.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0017","t":"A practitioner who publishes a fee for routine services must, according to Circular 230, adhere to that fee for at least how long after the last date of publication?","mc":{"s":"A practitioner who publishes a fee for routine services must, according to Circular 230, adhere to that fee for at least how long after the last date of publication?","d":"","o":["7 calendar days","30 calendar days","60 calendar days","90 calendar days"],"a":2,"cite":"C230 §10.30(b)","rat":"A practitioner who publishes fee information must adhere to the published fee for at least 30 calendar days after the last date of publication.","ex":["This is too short; the rule uses a 30-day period.","A practitioner who publishes fee information must adhere to the published fee for at least 30 calendar days after the last date of publication.","This is longer than the rule requires.","This is longer than the rule requires."],"wt":["limit","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner who publishes fees must adhere to them for at least 30 calendar days after the last publication date.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1001","t":"Nadia Ferro, CPA, is drafting a written opinion for Brightline Lodging LLC on whether the investors in its new hotel will be able to deduct the LLC's early-year losses. The exhibit lists four statements from the draft letter. Which statement would make the letter inconsistent with the standards for written tax advice if it stays in the final letter?","mc":{"s":"Nadia Ferro, CPA, is drafting a written opinion for Brightline Lodging LLC on whether the investors in its new hotel will be able to deduct the LLC's early-year losses. The exhibit lists four statements from the draft letter. Which statement would make the letter inconsistent with the standards for written tax advice if it stays in the final letter?","d":"","o":["Statement 1, the assumption about the completion date","Statement 2, the application of the loss rules to the stated facts","Statement 3, the acceptance of the occupancy projection","Statement 4, the reliance on the manager's statement about debt"],"a":3,"cite":"C230 §10.37; C230 §10.22","rat":"Written advice may not rely on a projection when the reliance is unreasonable.","ex":["A reasonable assumption about a future event, supported by a document the practitioner reviewed and disclosed in the letter, is allowed.","Relating the law and authorities to the facts is required in written advice, so this statement supports the letter.","Written advice may not rely on a projection when the reliance is unreasonable. Nadia's own workpapers contradict the 95% figure, so she ignored facts she knows and relied unreasonably on the promoter.","Reasonable reliance on a written statement from the client's manager is allowed when nothing in the file contradicts it."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Written tax advice may not rely on projections or assumptions that are unreasonable given facts the practitioner knows.","tb":[["No.","Statement in the draft letter"],["1","We assumed that construction will be finished by October 2026, because the loan agreement we reviewed requires completion by that date."],["2","We related the loss-limitation rules and the authorities we cite to the facts described in this letter and explained our reasoning."],["3","We accepted the promoter's projection of 95% occupancy without testing it, although our own workpapers show comparable hotels in the market averaging about 60%."],["4","We relied on the manager's written statement that the LLC has no debt other than the bank loan; nothing in the records we saw contradicts it."]],"from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1002","t":"Which situation presents a conflict of interest for Imani Okafor, CPA, that she may accept only if she reasonably believes she can serve every client competently and diligently and each affected client gives informed consent that is confirmed in writing?","mc":{"s":"Which situation presents a conflict of interest for Imani Okafor, CPA, that she may accept only if she reasonably believes she can serve every client competently and diligently and each affected client gives informed consent that is confirmed in writing?","d":"","o":["She represents two competing restaurants, each in a separate IRS examination that raises unrelated issues.","She represents a husband in an examination of a joint return and his wife in a request for relief from liability on that return.","She represents a client in the examination of a return she prepared, and the IRS has raised no question about her work.","She represents a corporation in an IRS matter and earlier prepared returns for its former owner in an unrelated engagement that has ended."],"a":2,"cite":"C230 §10.29","rat":"Relief for the wife would shift liability to the husband, so representing both is directly adverse.","ex":["Business rivalry does not make the clients' positions before the IRS directly adverse, and nothing limits her work for either client, so no conflict arises.","Relief for the wife would shift liability to the husband, so representing both is directly adverse. Imani may proceed only with each client's informed consent, confirmed in writing, and a reasonable belief that she can serve both competently and diligently.","A personal-interest conflict needs a significant risk that her own interest would materially limit the representation, such as a preparer penalty proposed against her. Nothing here suggests that.","A duty to a former client creates a conflict only if it materially limits the current representation. The earlier engagement is unrelated and finished."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Directly adverse client interests, such as spouses in innocent-spouse relief, need reasonable belief and informed written consent; mere business rivalry is not adversity.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1003","t":"Ravi Menon, EA, is asked to quote fees for IRS work. Under Circular 230, which arrangement is a contingent fee?","mc":{"s":"Ravi Menon, EA, is asked to quote fees for IRS work. Under Circular 230, which arrangement is a contingent fee?","d":"","o":["An hourly rate of $250, billed monthly for all of the time Ravi spends on the client's examination","A fee of 1% of the client's gross receipts, charged for monthly bookkeeping that is unrelated to any tax position","A fixed fee of $4,000 set in advance, payable half at signing and half on delivery of the completed return","A fixed fee of $4,000 for a return, refunded to the client if the IRS later disallows a position on it"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.27(c)(1)","rat":"A fee that is returned if a return position is not sustained depends on the result.","ex":["Time-based billing does not depend on the outcome of any position, so it is not a contingent fee.","A percentage fee is contingent only when it turns on whether a tax position is sustained or on a tax result. This fee depends on receipts for bookkeeping, not on a tax result.","A fee fixed in advance and payable on stated dates is not tied to any result, so it is not contingent.","A fee that is returned if a return position is not sustained depends on the result. Circular 230 treats reimbursement guarantees like this as contingent fees even though the stated fee is fixed."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under Circular 230 a fee that depends on the outcome, including one refundable if a position fails, is contingent.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1004","t":"During an examination of a client's 2024 return, a revenue agent makes a proper written request to Jorge Ibarra, CPA, for the client's 2024 payroll records. Neither Jorge nor the client has the records. The client says that a former payroll company holds them and refuses to release them to the client. What must Jorge do under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"During an examination of a client's 2024 return, a revenue agent makes a proper written request to Jorge Ibarra, CPA, for the client's 2024 payroll records. Neither Jorge nor the client has the records. The client says that a former payroll company holds them and refuses to release them to the client. What must Jorge do under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["Ask the client who holds the records, then promptly tell the agent that neither has them and name the company as the client reports","Say nothing more, because the IRS can summon the payroll company itself and Jorge has no duty to say who might hold the records","Contact the payroll company himself, confirm exactly what records it holds, and deliver copies of them to the revenue agent","Tell the agent that the records are privileged, because they were created for the client's business and not for use by the IRS"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.20(a)(2) (privilege exception: §10.20(a)(1))","rat":"When records are outside the control of both practitioner and client, the practitioner must promptly notify the IRS and give the identity of the holder as the client reports it, after reasonable inquiry of the client.","ex":["When records are outside the control of both practitioner and client, the practitioner must promptly notify the IRS and give the identity of the holder as the client reports it, after reasonable inquiry of the client.","The practitioner must promptly notify the IRS and share what he knows about who holds the records. The IRS's ability to issue a summons does not remove that duty.","The practitioner need only ask his client and pass on the answer. He is not required to make inquiry of anyone else or to verify what the client says.","The privilege exception applies only if the practitioner believes in good faith and on reasonable grounds that the records are privileged. Ordinary business records held by a third party are not."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"If records are held by neither practitioner nor client, the practitioner must promptly notify the IRS and identify the holder as the client reports.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1005","t":"In 2026, Carlos Vega, CPA, is preparing a client's 2025 return. He finds that the 2024 return he prepared reported a $200,000 net operating loss when the supportable loss is $50,000, because personal expenses were deducted. Carlos has explained the error and its consequences to the client, who refuses to amend the 2024 return and insists that the 2025 return use the full $200,000 loss carryover. What is Carlos's position under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"In 2026, Carlos Vega, CPA, is preparing a client's 2025 return. He finds that the 2024 return he prepared reported a $200,000 net operating loss when the supportable loss is $50,000, because personal expenses were deducted. Carlos has explained the error and its consequences to the client, who refuses to amend the 2024 return and insists that the 2025 return use the full $200,000 loss carryover. What is Carlos's position under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["He must report the 2024 error to the IRS before he may prepare any 2025 return for the client","He may sign the 2025 return with the full carryover, because once he advised the client his duty for the 2024 error ended","He must file an amended 2024 return himself, because he prepared it and his duty of care runs to the IRS","He need not notify the IRS, but he may not sign a 2025 return that claims a carryover he knows is unsupported"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.21; C230 §10.34; C230 §10.22","rat":"The duty for a known prior-year error is to advise the client of the error and its consequences; there is no duty to notify the IRS or to amend.","ex":["Circular 230 requires advising the client, not reporting to the IRS, and client confidentiality restricts disclosure.","Advising the client satisfies only the duty for the prior return. Carrying the known error into a new return means signing a position he knows is unsupported.","A practitioner cannot amend a client's return on his own, and Circular 230 does not require him to do so.","The duty for a known prior-year error is to advise the client of the error and its consequences; there is no duty to notify the IRS or to amend. But a practitioner may not sign a return containing a position he knows lacks a reasonable basis."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner who knows of a prior-year error must advise the client, need not tell the IRS, but may not sign a return carrying the unsupported position.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"2":[{"i":"REG-I-A-0018","t":"Dana Whitfield, a paid preparer, prepared a return for a fee of $3,400. The position she took lacked substantial authority and was not disclosed, and Dana cannot show reasonable cause and good faith. She did not act willfully or recklessly. What is Dana's penalty for the unreasonable position?","mc":{"s":"Dana Whitfield, a paid preparer, prepared a return for a fee of $3,400. The position she took lacked substantial authority and was not disclosed, and Dana cannot show reasonable cause and good faith. She did not act willfully or recklessly. What is Dana's penalty for the unreasonable position?","d":"","o":["$1,000","$1,700","$2,550","$5,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6694(a); Reg §1.6694-2","rat":"The penalty is the greater of $1,000 or 50% of the income derived from the return: 50% x $3,400 = $1,700, which is greater than $1,000.","ex":["Uses the $1,000 floor without checking the 50% alternative, which is larger here.","The penalty is the greater of $1,000 or 50% of the income derived from the return: 50% x $3,400 = $1,700, which is greater than $1,000.","Applies the 75% rate for willful or reckless conduct, which is not present.","Uses the $5,000 floor for willful or reckless conduct."],"wt":["calc","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Preparer penalty for an unreasonable position is the greater of $1,000 or 50% of the income derived from the return.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Unreasonable position, no willfulness: greater of $1,000 or 50% of income derived","50% x 3,400 = 1,700","max(1,000, 1,700) = 1,700"]}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0019","t":"A preparer earned $9,200 on a return. The IRS establishes that the preparer willfully understated the client's tax by disregarding the client's documents. Assuming only the willful-or-reckless preparer penalty is asserted, what is the penalty?","mc":{"s":"A preparer earned $9,200 on a return. The IRS establishes that the preparer willfully understated the client's tax by disregarding the client's documents. Assuming only the willful-or-reckless preparer penalty is asserted, what is the penalty?","d":"","o":["$4,600","$5,000","$6,900","$9,200"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6694(b)","rat":"For willful or reckless conduct the penalty is the greater of $5,000 or 75% of the income derived: 75% x $9,200 = $6,900.","ex":["Uses 50%, the rate for an unreasonable position, not willful conduct.","Uses only the $5,000 floor; 75% of the income is larger.","For willful or reckless conduct the penalty is the greater of $5,000 or 75% of the income derived: 75% x $9,200 = $6,900.","Takes the whole fee. The penalty is 75% of the fee, not 100%."],"wt":["limit","calc","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Preparer penalty for willful or reckless understatement is the greater of $5,000 or 75% of the income derived from the return.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Willful or reckless: greater of $5,000 or 75% of income derived","75% x 9,200 = 6,900","max(5,000, 6,900) = 6,900"]}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0020","t":"Which of the following persons is a tax return preparer who is subject to the preparer penalty provisions?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following persons is a tax return preparer who is subject to the preparer penalty provisions?","d":"","o":["A CPA paid to prepare most of a client's return who does not sign it","A clerical assistant who only types and copies a return prepared by others","An employee who prepares the employer's own return","A volunteer at a free clinic who prepares a return without compensation"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7701(a)(36); Reg §301.7701-15","rat":"A nonsigning preparer who prepares all or a substantial portion of a return for compensation is a tax return preparer.","ex":["A nonsigning preparer who prepares all or a substantial portion of a return for compensation is a tax return preparer.","Merely typing, reproducing or mechanical assistance does not make one a preparer.","Preparing the employer's own return, without being paid as a preparer, does not make one a preparer.","The definition requires preparation for compensation."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A tax return preparer is anyone paid to prepare all or a substantial portion of a return, signing or not.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0021","t":"Mateo Cruz prepared a client's return taking a position that is part of a reportable transaction. The position has substantial authority, but Mateo does not reasonably believe it would more likely than not be sustained. There was no willful or reckless conduct. What is the preparer penalty result?","mc":{"s":"Mateo Cruz prepared a client's return taking a position that is part of a reportable transaction. The position has substantial authority, but Mateo does not reasonably believe it would more likely than not be sustained. There was no willful or reckless conduct. What is the preparer penalty result?","d":"","o":["No preparer penalty applies, because the position has substantial authority and Mateo did not act willfully","The unreasonable-position penalty may apply; a more-likely-than-not belief is required","No preparer penalty applies if the position is disclosed on Form 8275","The willful-or-reckless penalty applies automatically"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6694(a)(2)(C); Reg §1.6694-2(c)(3)","rat":"For reportable transactions, substantial authority is not enough; the preparer must reasonably believe the position would more likely than not be sustained.","ex":["Substantial authority is the standard for ordinary undisclosed positions, not reportable transactions.","For reportable transactions, substantial authority is not enough; the preparer must reasonably believe the position would more likely than not be sustained.","Disclosure does not cure a reportable transaction position lacking the required belief.","That penalty requires willful or reckless conduct, which is not present."],"wt":["concept","","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For a reportable transaction, the preparer needs a reasonable more-likely-than-not belief; substantial authority alone does not avoid the unreasonable-position penalty.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0022","t":"A tax return preparer fails to give the taxpayer a copy of the completed return no later than when it is presented for signature. Which penalty provision applies?","mc":{"s":"A tax return preparer fails to give the taxpayer a copy of the completed return no later than when it is presented for signature. Which penalty provision applies?","d":"","o":["The penalty for an understatement due to an unreasonable position","The criminal penalty for disclosing return information","The penalty for not furnishing the taxpayer a copy of the return","The penalty for failing to file an information return for wages"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6107(a); IRC §6695(a)","rat":"Preparers must give the taxpayer a copy of the return, and failing to do so triggers a per-return penalty.","ex":["This requires an understatement caused by an unreasonable position, which is not described.","The facts do not involve disclosure or use of information.","Preparers must give the taxpayer a copy of the return, and failing to do so triggers a per-return penalty.","The facts involve a preparer's copy duty, not wage reporting."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A preparer must give the taxpayer a copy of the return no later than when it is presented for signature, or a per-return penalty applies.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0023","t":"Jorge Salas prepares a return that claims the earned income credit and the child tax credit. He asks only a few routine questions, does not complete Form 8867 or the credit worksheets, and keeps no record of his inquiries. The credits are ultimately allowed correctly. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Jorge Salas prepares a return that claims the earned income credit and the child tax credit. He asks only a few routine questions, does not complete Form 8867 or the credit worksheets, and keeps no record of his inquiries. The credits are ultimately allowed correctly. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["No penalty applies, because the credits were correct","A penalty applies only if the IRS proves fraud","A penalty applies only if the IRS shows that Jorge prepared more than 25 such returns that year","A due diligence penalty may apply even though the credits were correct"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6695(g); Reg §1.6695-2; Form 8867 instructions","rat":"The due diligence penalty depends on the preparer's failure to comply with the requirements, not on whether the credit turns out to be correct.","ex":["The penalty arises from noncompliance with the due diligence rules regardless of the credit's accuracy.","Fraud is not required for the due diligence penalty.","There is no minimum number of returns.","The due diligence penalty depends on the preparer's failure to comply with the requirements, not on whether the credit turns out to be correct."],"wt":["trap","concept","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The due diligence penalty for EITC, CTC and similar credits applies for noncompliance with the requirements even when the credits are correct.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0024","t":"Which disclosure of a client's tax return information by a preparer generally does NOT require the client's written consent?","mc":{"s":"Which disclosure of a client's tax return information by a preparer generally does NOT require the client's written consent?","d":"","o":["Disclosure to another U.S. preparer who is helping prepare the return","Disclosure to the preparer's affiliated insurance agency to market products","Disclosure to a lender who will use it to approve a mortgage for the client","Disclosure to a data vendor that will sell the client's information"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7216; Reg §301.7216-2; Rev. Proc. 2013-14","rat":"A preparer may disclose return information to another U.S.","ex":["A preparer may disclose return information to another U.S. preparer to help prepare the return without the taxpayer's consent.","Using return information for marketing requires the taxpayer's consent.","Disclosure to a lender or other third party requires consent.","Selling or using return information for non-tax purposes requires consent."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A preparer may disclose return information to another U.S. preparer helping prepare the return without consent; other disclosures and uses need written consent.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0025","t":"A staff preparer at Andre Novak's firm improperly disclosed clients' return information to 60 different outsiders during the year. Ignoring any criminal action, what civil penalty applies to the preparer for the year? The penalty is $250 per disclosure, with an annual cap of $10,000.","mc":{"s":"A staff preparer at Andre Novak's firm improperly disclosed clients' return information to 60 different outsiders during the year. Ignoring any criminal action, what civil penalty applies to the preparer for the year? The penalty is $250 per disclosure, with an annual cap of $10,000.","d":"","o":["$250","$1,000","$10,000","$15,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6713; IRC §7216","rat":"The civil penalty is $250 for each disclosure or use, capped at $10,000 per year: 60 x $250 = $15,000, limited to $10,000.","ex":["Applies the penalty once, not per disclosure.","Uses the criminal fine limit for unauthorized disclosure instead of the civil penalty.","The civil penalty is $250 for each disclosure or use, capped at $10,000 per year: 60 x $250 = $15,000, limited to $10,000.","Multiplies out the disclosures without applying the $10,000 annual cap."],"wt":["calc","limit","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The civil penalty for unauthorized disclosure or use of return information is $250 per disclosure, capped at $10,000 per year.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Penalty = 250 per disclosure or use","60 x 250 = 15,000","Annual cap 10,000, so min(15,000, 10,000) = 10,000"]}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0026","t":"For how long must a tax return preparer keep a copy of each return prepared, or a list of the taxpayers?","mc":{"s":"For how long must a tax return preparer keep a copy of each return prepared, or a list of the taxpayers?","d":"","o":["1 year after the close of the return period","3 years after the close of the return period","6 years after the close of the return period","7 years after the close of the return period"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6107(b); IRC §6695(d)","rat":"A preparer must retain a copy of the return, or a list with each taxpayer's name and identification number, for 3 years after the return period ends.","ex":["This is shorter than the 3-year requirement.","A preparer must retain a copy of the return, or a list with each taxpayer's name and identification number, for 3 years after the return period ends.","This is longer than the requirement.","This is longer than the requirement."],"wt":["limit","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A preparer must retain a copy of each return or a list of taxpayers for 3 years after the close of the return period.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0027","t":"Which of the following is a felony under the Internal Revenue Code?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is a felony under the Internal Revenue Code?","d":"","o":["Willfully failing to file a required return","Knowingly making an unauthorized disclosure of return information to a third party for a fee","Willfully helping prepare a return known to be false as to a material matter","Negligently understating a client's tax"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §7206(2); IRC §7203; IRC §7216","rat":"Aiding or assisting in a false return is a felony carrying up to three years in prison.","ex":["This is a misdemeanor, punishable by up to one year in prison.","This is a misdemeanor under the preparer confidentiality provision.","Aiding or assisting in a false return is a felony carrying up to three years in prison.","Negligence gives rise to civil penalties, not criminal liability."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Willfully aiding or assisting in preparing a materially false return is a felony; failure to file and unauthorized disclosure are misdemeanors.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1006","t":"Lena and Tomas each prepared a client's return claiming the same $70,000 deduction, as shown in the exhibit. Neither return involves a tax shelter or reportable transaction, neither preparer acted willfully or recklessly, and the IRS disallows both deductions. Which statement about the preparer penalty for an unreasonable position is correct?","mc":{"s":"Lena and Tomas each prepared a client's return claiming the same $70,000 deduction, as shown in the exhibit. Neither return involves a tax shelter or reportable transaction, neither preparer acted willfully or recklessly, and the IRS disallows both deductions. Which statement about the preparer penalty for an unreasonable position is correct?","d":"","o":["Only Tomas is subject to the penalty, because disclosing a reasonable-basis position protects the preparer","Neither preparer is subject to the penalty, because the position had a reasonable basis in both of the returns","Both preparers are subject to the penalty, because the position lacked substantial authority in both of the returns","Only Lena is subject to the penalty, because her disclosure shows that she knew the position was weak when she filed"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6694(a); Reg §1.6694-2","rat":"A preparer is not penalized for a position that has a reasonable basis and is adequately disclosed.","ex":["A preparer is not penalized for a position that has a reasonable basis and is adequately disclosed. Lena disclosed. Tomas took the same position without substantial authority or disclosure and has no reasonable cause.","A reasonable basis protects a preparer only when the position is also adequately disclosed. Tomas did not disclose, so it does not protect him.","Lack of substantial authority is enough only for an undisclosed position. Lena's disclosure of a position with a reasonable basis avoids the penalty.","Disclosure is not evidence of wrongdoing. It is the step that protects a preparer whose position has a reasonable basis."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A reasonable-basis position avoids the preparer penalty only if adequately disclosed; otherwise substantial authority is needed.","tb":[["Preparer","Position taken","Disclosed on Form 8275?","Reasonable cause shown?"],["Lena","$70,000 deduction; reasonable basis but no substantial authority","Yes, attached to the return","No"],["Tomas","Same $70,000 deduction; same support","No","No"]],"from":"2026-01-01"}}],"3":[{"i":"REG-I-A-0028","t":"Trina Adams, CPA, prepared a client's 2024 return. In 2026 she learns that the return omitted $40,000 of income. The client refuses to file an amended return. Under AICPA tax standards, what should Trina do?","mc":{"s":"Trina Adams, CPA, prepared a client's 2024 return. In 2026 she learns that the return omitted $40,000 of income. The client refuses to file an amended return. Under AICPA tax standards, what should Trina do?","d":"","o":["Report the error to the IRS, since she prepared the original return","Take no action, because the return was filed in a prior year","Amend the return herself and send it to the IRS without the client's approval, since she prepared it","Advise the client of the error and recommend correction; consider whether to continue"],"a":4,"cite":"SSTS No. 1 §1.2 (2024); legacy No. 6; AICPA Code 1.700.001","rat":"The member should promptly inform the client of the error and its consequences and recommend corrective action, but is not required to disclose the error to the IRS.","ex":["A member may not disclose confidential information to the IRS without client consent, unless a legal exception applies.","The duty to advise the client applies once the member becomes aware of the error.","The client must sign the return; the member cannot file it unilaterally.","The member should promptly inform the client of the error and its consequences and recommend corrective action, but is not required to disclose the error to the IRS."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under AICPA standards a member who learns of a prior-year client error must advise the client and recommend correction but need not tell the IRS.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0029","t":"A client's business records were destroyed in a flood. The client gives CPA Sam Lee a reasonable estimate of the year's supply costs. Under AICPA tax standards, how may Sam treat the estimate?","mc":{"s":"A client's business records were destroyed in a flood. The client gives CPA Sam Lee a reasonable estimate of the year's supply costs. Under AICPA tax standards, how may Sam treat the estimate?","d":"","o":["Use it if reasonable, but do not present it as exact","Refuse to use any estimate, because tax returns must contain exact figures","Use the estimate and label it as exact so the return looks consistent","Round every estimate up to the nearest $10,000 to protect the client"],"a":1,"cite":"SSTS Use of Estimates (legacy No. 4); SSTS No. 2 §2.4 (2024, secondary source); legacy No. 4","rat":"A member may use a client's reasonable estimates when exact data are impractical to obtain, provided they are not presented as precise amounts.","ex":["A member may use a client's reasonable estimates when exact data are impractical to obtain, provided they are not presented as precise amounts.","Estimates are allowed when records are unavailable and the estimate is reasonable.","Presenting an estimate as exact would be misleading.","An estimate must be reasonable, not padded."],"wt":["","trap","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A member may use a client's reasonable estimates when exact data are impractical to obtain, provided they are not presented as exact.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0030","t":"Which reason does NOT justify a member's leaving an answer on a return blank, according to the AICPA's tax standards?","mc":{"s":"Which reason does NOT justify a member's leaving an answer on a return blank, according to the AICPA's tax standards?","d":"","o":["The information needed to answer is not available and the answer is not significant","The client wants the answer omitted to reduce audit risk","The question is unclear and reasonable people could interpret it differently","The answer's effect on the tax liability is immaterial"],"a":2,"cite":"SSTS Answers to Questions on Returns (legacy No. 2); SSTS No. 2 §2.2 (2024, secondary source); legacy No. 2","rat":"Avoiding audit risk is not a valid reason.","ex":["Omission can be reasonable when information is unavailable and the omission is not material.","Avoiding audit risk is not a valid reason. A member should make reasonable efforts to obtain answers, and may omit one only for reasons such as unavailable information or reasonable uncertainty.","A genuine uncertainty about the question's meaning can justify omission.","Immateriality can justify omission."],"wt":["trap","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A member may leave a return answer blank only for unavailable information, reasonable uncertainty or immateriality, not to reduce audit risk.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0031","t":"Reviewing a client's 2025 data, CPA Wei Zhang notices that the client's 2024 return reported $18,000 of rental income, but no rental income appears this year and the client did not say the property was sold. What do AICPA tax standards suggest?","mc":{"s":"Reviewing a client's 2025 data, CPA Wei Zhang notices that the client's 2024 return reported $18,000 of rental income, but no rental income appears this year and the client did not say the property was sold. What do AICPA tax standards suggest?","d":"","o":["Omit the rental income, because the client did not provide it","Report the same $18,000 of rental income without asking anything","Ask the client about the change from the prior year","Withdraw from the engagement immediately without asking"],"a":3,"cite":"SSTS Certain Procedural Aspects of Preparing Returns (legacy No. 3); SSTS No. 2 (2024) preparation-procedures section; section number not confirmed; legacy No. 3","rat":"A member may use prior returns and should make reasonable inquiries when information appears incorrect, incomplete or inconsistent.","ex":["The member cannot ignore information that suggests income was left out.","The member should inquire rather than assume.","A member may use prior returns and should make reasonable inquiries when information appears incorrect, incomplete or inconsistent.","A difference calls for an inquiry, not automatic withdrawal."],"wt":["concept","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A member may use prior returns but should make reasonable inquiries when client information appears incorrect, incomplete or inconsistent.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0032","t":"CPA Renata Costa gave a client written advice on structuring a transaction. A later law change affects that advice. Under AICPA tax standards, is Renata obligated to update the client?","mc":{"s":"CPA Renata Costa gave a client written advice on structuring a transaction. A later law change affects that advice. Under AICPA tax standards, is Renata obligated to update the client?","d":"","o":["Yes, for as long as the client remains a client of the firm","Yes, but only if the law change increases the client's tax","Yes, within 30 days, and she must also notify the IRS","No, unless she agreed to do so or is helping implement the advice"],"a":4,"cite":"Form and Content of Advice to Taxpayers (legacy No. 7); SSTS No. 3 §3.1.5 (2024, secondary source); legacy No. 7","rat":"A member has no duty to communicate later developments that affect prior advice unless engaged for that purpose or assisting in implementation.","ex":["The duty to update exists only by agreement or when assisting with implementation.","The direction of the effect does not create the duty.","There is no such rule, and notifying the IRS would breach confidentiality.","A member has no duty to communicate later developments that affect prior advice unless engaged for that purpose or assisting in implementation."],"wt":["trap","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A member has no duty to update prior advice for later law changes unless agreed or assisting in implementation.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0033","t":"In 2024 the IRS Appeals Office agreed, in an examination of a client's 2022 return, that the client's vehicle was used 75% for business. For 2025 the facts support only about 40% business use. Which statement best reflects AICPA tax standards?","mc":{"s":"In 2024 the IRS Appeals Office agreed, in an examination of a client's 2022 return, that the client's vehicle was used 75% for business. For 2025 the facts support only about 40% business use. Which statement best reflects AICPA tax standards?","d":"","o":["The member is not bound by the earlier result and may take the position the facts support for 2025","The member must keep the 75% for 2025 because the IRS accepted that percentage in the earlier examination","The member must report 40% and also amend the 2022 return","The member must disclose the difference on Form 8275"],"a":1,"cite":"SSTS Departure from a Position Previously Concluded (legacy No. 5); SSTS No. 2 §2.5 (2024, secondary source); legacy No. 5","rat":"A member is not bound to follow a position resolved in an administrative proceeding for a prior year unless a doctrine such as a closing agreement or estoppel applies.","ex":["A member is not bound to follow a position resolved in an administrative proceeding for a prior year unless a doctrine such as a closing agreement or estoppel applies.","An earlier settlement does not bind a different tax year absent an agreement or estoppel.","Nothing requires amending the earlier year as a result.","No disclosure is required merely because a position differs from a prior year's resolution."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A member is not bound to a prior-year administrative result for a different year absent a closing agreement or estoppel, and follows the facts.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-0034","t":"In which situation may a CPA disclose confidential client information without the client's consent, according to the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?","mc":{"s":"In which situation may a CPA disclose confidential client information without the client's consent, according to the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?","d":"","o":["To a lender who asks whether the client is a reliable borrower","In response to a valid and enforceable subpoena or summons","To another client who is a competitor and asks for the information","To a marketing firm that helps grow the CPA's practice"],"a":2,"cite":"AICPA Code 1.700.001; IRC §7216","rat":"The Code permits disclosure needed to comply with a valid, enforceable subpoena or summons, and some other legal requirements.","ex":["Disclosure to a lender needs consent.","The Code permits disclosure needed to comply with a valid, enforceable subpoena or summons, and some other legal requirements.","A member may not share one client's information with others without consent.","Using client information for marketing requires consent."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A CPA may disclose confidential client information without consent to comply with a valid, enforceable subpoena or summons.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1007","t":"Halden & Pryor, CPAs, audit the financial statements of Kestrel Products Inc. and also provide tax services to Kestrel. Which tax service would impair the firm's independence for the audit under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?","mc":{"s":"Halden & Pryor, CPAs, audit the financial statements of Kestrel Products Inc. and also provide tax services to Kestrel. Which tax service would impair the firm's independence for the audit under the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct?","d":"","o":["Preparing Kestrel's federal return from records its staff assembled, after which its chief financial officer reviews, approves and signs it","Deciding for Kestrel, without its approval, which of two supportable positions its return will take and directing the staff to file it","Advising Kestrel on the federal tax effects of a proposed acquisition while its board decides whether to proceed","Preparing an amended return for Kestrel for a flat fee agreed in advance and not tied to any refund"],"a":2,"cite":"AICPA Code 1.295.030.01-.02 (management responsibilities); AICPA Code 1.295.160.03 (tax services) and 1.295.040.01 (general requirements for nonattest services); AICPA Code 1.510.001.01(b), .03 (contingent fees rule)","rat":"Making decisions that belong to management is assuming a management responsibility, which impairs independence for an attest client.","ex":["Return preparation does not impair independence when management makes the decisions and takes responsibility for the return, as it does here.","Making decisions that belong to management is assuming a management responsibility, which impairs independence for an attest client.","Tax planning advice is allowed when management makes the decision, and here the board makes it.","A contingent fee for preparing an amended return would violate the Contingent Fees Rule. This fee is fixed and not tied to any result, so the service is permitted."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Making decisions that belong to management for an attest client impairs independence; return preparation with management approval does not.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-A-1008","t":"Which disclosure of confidential client information by a CPA in public practice would violate the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct because it is made without the client's specific consent?","mc":{"s":"Which disclosure of confidential client information by a CPA in public practice would violate the AICPA Code of Professional Conduct because it is made without the client's specific consent?","d":"","o":["Answering a written inquiry from the state board of accountancy that is investigating a complaint about the CPA's work for the client","Giving client files to a reviewer who is performing a peer review of the CPA's practice under an authorized review program","Telling a banker friend, who asked informally about the client's creditworthiness, that the client's profits fell last year","Complying with a validly issued and enforceable subpoena from a court in a civil suit against the client"],"a":3,"cite":"AICPA Code 1.700.001","rat":"Disclosing confidential client information to a third party for that party's use requires the client's specific consent.","ex":["The Code allows a member to respond to inquiries from a duly constituted investigative or disciplinary body without client consent.","Review of a member's practice under an authorized program is an exception, and the reviewer must keep the information confidential.","Disclosing confidential client information to a third party for that party's use requires the client's specific consent. No exception covers an informal answer to a bank contact.","A valid, enforceable subpoena is an exception, so compliance does not violate the confidentiality rule."],"wt":["trap","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A CPA may disclose confidential client information without consent to a board inquiry, peer review or enforceable subpoena, not to an informal third-party inquiry.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"4":[{"i":"REG-I-B-0001","t":"Which Circular 230 sanction is a public reprimand that does not by itself remove the practitioner's right to practice?","mc":{"s":"Which Circular 230 sanction is a public reprimand that does not by itself remove the practitioner's right to practice?","d":"","o":["Suspension","Disbarment","Censure","Expedited suspension"],"a":3,"cite":"C230 §10.50","rat":"Censure is a public reprimand and leaves the right to practice intact.","ex":["Suspension bars the practitioner from practice for a period of time.","Disbarment bars the practitioner from practice, and reinstatement can be sought only after a waiting period.","Censure is a public reprimand and leaves the right to practice intact. Suspension and disbarment remove practice rights, and a monetary penalty is a fine.","This is a procedure for quickly suspending a practitioner after certain convictions or licensing actions, not a public reprimand."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Censure is a public reprimand that leaves the practitioner's right to practice intact, unlike suspension or disbarment.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0002","t":"Which office administers and enforces the standards of practice for those who represent taxpayers before the IRS under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Which office administers and enforces the standards of practice for those who represent taxpayers before the IRS under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["The Treasury Inspector General for Tax Administration","The IRS Taxpayer Advocate Service","The IRS Criminal Investigation Division","The IRS Office of Professional Responsibility"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.1; C230 §10.60","rat":"The Office of Professional Responsibility interprets Circular 230, and investigates and pursues sanctions against practitioners.","ex":["TIGTA oversees IRS operations and employee conduct; it does not run the practitioner discipline program.","The Taxpayer Advocate Service helps taxpayers resolve problems with the IRS; it does not discipline practitioners.","Criminal Investigation investigates tax crimes; it does not administer Circular 230.","The Office of Professional Responsibility interprets Circular 230, and investigates and pursues sanctions against practitioners."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The IRS Office of Professional Responsibility administers Circular 230 and pursues sanctions against practitioners.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0003","t":"Following a disciplinary proceeding, the Secretary of the Treasury may impose a monetary penalty on a practitioner. The practitioner earned $12,000 in fees from the conduct that is sanctionable. What is the maximum monetary penalty?","mc":{"s":"Following a disciplinary proceeding, the Secretary of the Treasury may impose a monetary penalty on a practitioner. The practitioner earned $12,000 in fees from the conduct that is sanctionable. What is the maximum monetary penalty?","d":"","o":["$12,000, the gross income derived from the conduct","$6,000, half of the gross income derived from the conduct","$24,000, twice the gross income derived from the conduct","$5,000, a fixed statutory amount for every practitioner"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.50(c)","rat":"The monetary penalty may not exceed the gross income derived, or to be derived, from the conduct giving rise to the penalty.","ex":["The monetary penalty may not exceed the gross income derived, or to be derived, from the conduct giving rise to the penalty.","Confuses the limit with the 50% measure under the preparer penalty for unreasonable positions.","The cap is not a multiple of the income derived.","Circular 230 sets no fixed amount; the cap depends on the income derived."],"wt":["","concept","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A Circular 230 monetary penalty may not exceed the gross income derived from the sanctionable conduct.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0004","t":"Which conduct by a practitioner is disreputable conduct that can lead to sanction under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Which conduct by a practitioner is disreputable conduct that can lead to sanction under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["Advising a client on a position later disallowed by the IRS after reasonable inquiry and good-faith effort","Willfully failing to file the practitioner's own federal tax return when it was due","Quoting fees for routine services in a truthful advertisement","Withdrawing from an engagement after the client refuses to correct an error"],"a":2,"cite":"C230 §10.51(a)(6)","rat":"Willfully failing to file one's own federal return, or evading a federal tax, is listed as incompetence and disreputable conduct.","ex":["An unsuccessful position, taken competently and with due diligence, is not disreputable conduct.","Willfully failing to file one's own federal return, or evading a federal tax, is listed as incompetence and disreputable conduct.","Fee information may be published if it is not false or misleading.","Withdrawal may be appropriate and is not misconduct."],"wt":["trap","","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Willfully failing to file one's own federal return is disreputable conduct; an unsuccessful but competent position is not.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0005","t":"In 2026 Vikram Rao, an enrolled agent, is convicted of a federal crime involving dishonesty. The conviction occurred within the past five years. Which statement about the IRS's ability to act against him is correct?","mc":{"s":"In 2026 Vikram Rao, an enrolled agent, is convicted of a federal crime involving dishonesty. The conviction occurred within the past five years. Which statement about the IRS's ability to act against him is correct?","d":"","o":["Nothing can be done until Vikram has exhausted all appeals of the criminal case and any related civil claims","The IRS may act only if the crime was a tax offense","The IRS may use the expedited suspension procedure instead of the full hearing process","The IRS must first obtain a court order from the Tax Court"],"a":3,"cite":"C230 §10.82","rat":"Circular 230 permits expedited suspension of a practitioner convicted of certain crimes within the prior five years, including crimes involving dishonesty or breach of trust.","ex":["Expedited suspension can proceed once a conviction has occurred, whether or not an appeal is pending.","Crimes involving dishonesty or breach of trust qualify even if they are not tax crimes.","Circular 230 permits expedited suspension of a practitioner convicted of certain crimes within the prior five years, including crimes involving dishonesty or breach of trust.","The Tax Court has no role in practitioner discipline."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner convicted within five years of a crime involving dishonesty or breach of trust may be suspended by the expedited procedure, appeals notwithstanding.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0006","t":"After an administrative law judge decides a disciplinary case under Circular 230, to whom may either party appeal the decision?","mc":{"s":"After an administrative law judge decides a disciplinary case under Circular 230, to whom may either party appeal the decision?","d":"","o":["The United States Tax Court","The Court of Federal Claims","The IRS Independent Office of Appeals","The Secretary of the Treasury"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.77; C230 §10.78","rat":"The decision may be appealed to the Secretary of the Treasury, whose decision may then be reviewed in federal court.","ex":["The Tax Court does not hear practitioner discipline appeals.","The appeal is first administrative, to the Secretary, not to this court.","IRS Appeals handles taxpayer disputes, not appeals from practitioner discipline decisions.","The decision may be appealed to the Secretary of the Treasury, whose decision may then be reviewed in federal court."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An ALJ's decision in a Circular 230 disciplinary case is appealed to the Secretary of the Treasury.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0007","t":"After the Secretary of the Treasury disbars a practitioner under Circular 230, how long must the practitioner wait before applying for reinstatement?","mc":{"s":"After the Secretary of the Treasury disbars a practitioner under Circular 230, how long must the practitioner wait before applying for reinstatement?","d":"","o":["5 years","1 year","3 years","10 years"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.81","rat":"A disbarred practitioner may apply for reinstatement only after 5 years.","ex":["A disbarred practitioner may apply for reinstatement only after 5 years.","One year is too short under Circular 230.","Three years is too short under Circular 230.","Ten years is longer than the rule requires."],"wt":["","limit","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner disbarred under Circular 230 may apply for reinstatement only after 5 years.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0008","t":"Anita Bose holds a state CPA license and is also an enrolled agent. The state board revokes her CPA license for fraud. What is the consequence under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Anita Bose holds a state CPA license and is also an enrolled agent. The state board revokes her CPA license for fraud. What is the consequence under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["None, because state discipline has no effect on federal practice rights","She can no longer practice as a CPA before the IRS; the revocation may support sanction","Her enrolled agent status is automatically revoked in the same hour","She may continue practicing as a CPA before the IRS if she gives written notice within 30 days"],"a":2,"cite":"C230 §10.3(b); C230 §10.51(a)","rat":"CPAs practice before the IRS only while in good standing, and losing a professional license for misconduct can itself be a ground for sanction.","ex":["A CPA must be in good standing with the state, and license loss can support IRS discipline.","CPAs practice before the IRS only while in good standing, and losing a professional license for misconduct can itself be a ground for sanction.","Enrollment is not automatically revoked; the IRS must act.","Notice does not restore good standing."],"wt":["concept","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Loss of a state CPA license for misconduct ends CPA practice rights before the IRS and may support sanction, but does not automatically revoke enrolled agent status.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0009","t":"Which of the following results in the automatic suspension or termination of a member's AICPA membership, without a hearing, according to the AICPA bylaws?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following results in the automatic suspension or termination of a member's AICPA membership, without a hearing, according to the AICPA bylaws?","d":"","o":["An IRS accuracy-related penalty assessed on a client of the member","An arrest on a tax charge that ends in an acquittal","A conviction for aiding in a false tax return","A malpractice suit that the member settles without admitting fault"],"a":3,"cite":"AICPA Bylaws (automatic suspension/termination provision; current section number to be confirmed; formerly §730); AICPA Code 1.400","rat":"The bylaws provide for automatic action after certain convictions, including crimes involving false tax returns and crimes punishable by imprisonment for more than one year.","ex":["A civil penalty on a client does not itself trigger automatic termination.","Automatic action requires a conviction, not an arrest or acquittal.","The bylaws provide for automatic action after certain convictions, including crimes involving false tax returns and crimes punishable by imprisonment for more than one year.","A civil settlement does not trigger automatic termination."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"AICPA membership is terminated automatically after conviction of certain crimes such as aiding a false return, not after arrest, acquittal or civil settlement.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-0010","t":"Frank Ellis, CPA, is a practitioner who is engaged in a pending IRS examination. He asks a former enrolled agent, who has been disbarred from practice before the IRS, to advise on the strategy. Which statement is correct under Circular 230?","mc":{"s":"Frank Ellis, CPA, is a practitioner who is engaged in a pending IRS examination. He asks a former enrolled agent, who has been disbarred from practice before the IRS, to advise on the strategy. Which statement is correct under Circular 230?","d":"","o":["Frank may accept the assistance because the former agent is not paid by the IRS","Frank may accept it if the client consents in writing","Frank may accept it if the former agent does not appear before the IRS","Frank may not knowingly accept help from a disbarred person in an IRS matter"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.24","rat":"A practitioner may not knowingly accept assistance from, or assist, a person who is disbarred or suspended if that assistance relates to a matter before the IRS.","ex":["Who pays the person is irrelevant; the rule bars the assistance.","Client consent does not lift the prohibition.","The bar covers assistance in the matter, not only appearances.","A practitioner may not knowingly accept assistance from, or assist, a person who is disbarred or suspended if that assistance relates to a matter before the IRS."],"wt":["trap","trap","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A practitioner may not knowingly accept assistance from a disbarred or suspended person in an IRS matter, regardless of consent or appearance.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-1001","t":"The IRS finds that Marcus Hale, a CPA employed by Dunmore & Lyle, prepared documents for an IRS matter that willfully misstated facts, and that the firm earned $30,000 in fees for the work. The Secretary of the Treasury proceeds against Marcus. Which statement about a monetary penalty on the firm itself is correct?","mc":{"s":"The IRS finds that Marcus Hale, a CPA employed by Dunmore & Lyle, prepared documents for an IRS matter that willfully misstated facts, and that the firm earned $30,000 in fees for the work. The Secretary of the Treasury proceeds against Marcus. Which statement about a monetary penalty on the firm itself is correct?","d":"","o":["The firm may be fined up to $30,000, its fees, if it knew or reasonably should have known of Marcus's conduct","The firm may be fined up to $30,000 in every case, because it profited from the fees earned on the work","The firm cannot be fined, because a monetary penalty may be imposed only on the individual practitioner who acted","The firm may be fined up to $60,000, twice its fees, if it knew of Marcus's conduct and profited from it"],"a":1,"cite":"C230 §10.50(c)(1)(ii), (c)(2)","rat":"The Secretary may impose a monetary penalty on an employer or firm if it knew or reasonably should have known of the conduct.","ex":["The Secretary may impose a monetary penalty on an employer or firm if it knew or reasonably should have known of the conduct. The penalty is capped at the gross income derived from the conduct, here $30,000.","Earning the fees is not enough. The firm must have known, or reasonably should have known, of the conduct.","The rules expressly allow a penalty on the employer, firm or entity on whose behalf the practitioner acted.","The cap is the gross income derived or to be derived from the conduct, not a multiple of it."],"wt":["","concept","concept","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A firm may be fined up to the gross income from the conduct if it knew or reasonably should have known of the practitioner's conduct.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-B-1002","t":"The IRS establishes that Priya Nair, a CPA, willfully understated the tax on 40 client returns she prepared. Her state board of accountancy has already suspended her license for one year for the same conduct. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"The IRS establishes that Priya Nair, a CPA, willfully understated the tax on 40 client returns she prepared. Her state board of accountancy has already suspended her license for one year for the same conduct. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The IRS must wait until the suspension ends, because only one authority may discipline the same practitioner at a time","The IRS is barred from any further action over the same conduct, because a second penalty would be double jeopardy","The IRS may act only by asking the state board to lengthen the suspension, because states have exclusive power over CPAs","The IRS may still assess preparer penalties and seek sanctions, and the state action may itself support discipline"],"a":4,"cite":"C230 §10.50; C230 §10.51; IRC §6694(b)","rat":"The proceedings are independent.","ex":["Each authority acts under its own rules and no single-forum rule exists, so the IRS need not wait for the state board.","Administrative and civil penalties and sanctions by different authorities are separate proceedings. The state action does not bar them.","The IRS regulates practice before the IRS and enforces preparer penalties. The state's licensing power is not exclusive.","The proceedings are independent. The IRS can pursue its own preparer penalties and sanctions, and a state suspension is itself conduct that can support IRS discipline."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"State board discipline does not bar independent IRS preparer penalties and Circular 230 sanctions for the same conduct.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"5":[{"i":"REG-I-C-0001","t":"The IRS issues an administrative summons to a taxpayer, asking her to appear and produce books and records for an examination. She ignores it. What is the IRS's next step to compel compliance?","mc":{"s":"The IRS issues an administrative summons to a taxpayer, asking her to appear and produce books and records for an examination. She ignores it. What is the IRS's next step to compel compliance?","d":"","o":["Assess a deficiency and collect it without further procedure","Petition a federal district court to enforce the summons","Ask the Tax Court to enforce the summons","Treat the summons as satisfied after 90 days of silence"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7602; IRC §7604","rat":"A summons is not self-enforcing.","ex":["A summons is an information-gathering tool. Ignoring it does not by itself permit assessment.","A summons is not self-enforcing. The IRS must ask a U.S. district court to enforce it, and failure to obey a court order may lead to contempt.","The Tax Court has no authority to enforce IRS summonses.","Silence does not satisfy a summons."],"wt":["concept","","concept","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An IRS summons is not self-enforcing; the IRS must petition a federal district court to enforce it.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0002","t":"The IRS serves a summons on Brightwater Bank for records of Nadia Petrov's accounts, and gives Nadia proper notice of the summons. What is the deadline for Nadia to begin a proceeding to quash the summons, measured from the date notice was given to her?","mc":{"s":"The IRS serves a summons on Brightwater Bank for records of Nadia Petrov's accounts, and gives Nadia proper notice of the summons. What is the deadline for Nadia to begin a proceeding to quash the summons, measured from the date notice was given to her?","d":"","o":["10 days","20 days","30 days","90 days"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7609(b)(2)","rat":"A taxpayer who receives notice of a third-party summons has 20 days from the date the notice is given to begin a proceeding to quash it.","ex":["Ten days is shorter than the statutory period.","A taxpayer who receives notice of a third-party summons has 20 days from the date the notice is given to begin a proceeding to quash it.","Thirty days is longer than the statutory period.","Ninety days is the period to petition the Tax Court after a notice of deficiency, not the period to quash a summons."],"wt":["limit","","limit","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A taxpayer notified of a third-party summons has 20 days from the notice date to begin a proceeding to quash it.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0003","t":"The IRS schedules an in-person examination interview with Anil Mehta about his 2024 return. Which statement is correct about his right to record the interview?","mc":{"s":"The IRS schedules an in-person examination interview with Anil Mehta about his 2024 return. Which statement is correct about his right to record the interview?","d":"","o":["He may not record, because only the IRS may record interviews","He may record without any notice, as long as the examiner is told before the interview","He may make an audio recording if he gives the IRS 10 days advance written notice","He may record only if he is represented by a CPA"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §7521(a); Reg §301.7521-1; Pub 1; Pub 556","rat":"A taxpayer may record an in-person interview by giving at least 10 days advance written notice, at his own expense.","ex":["Taxpayers may record on proper notice.","Advance written notice is required.","A taxpayer may record an in-person interview by giving at least 10 days advance written notice, at his own expense.","Representation is not a condition to the right to record."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A taxpayer may audio record an in-person IRS interview with 10 days advance written notice.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0004","t":"Talia Brooks filed her 2023 individual income tax return on February 20, 2024. The return was due April 15, 2024, and she did not extend it. There was no fraud and no substantial omission of income. What is the last day the IRS can assess additional tax for 2023 under the general rule?","mc":{"s":"Talia Brooks filed her 2023 individual income tax return on February 20, 2024. The return was due April 15, 2024, and she did not extend it. There was no fraud and no substantial omission of income. What is the last day the IRS can assess additional tax for 2023 under the general rule?","d":"","o":["February 20, 2027","April 15, 2030","There is no limit","April 15, 2027"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6501(a); IRC §6501(b)(1)","rat":"A return filed before the due date is treated as filed on the due date.","ex":["Counts three years from the actual early filing date. Early returns are treated as filed on the due date.","Counts six years from the due date, which applies only if more than 25% of gross income is omitted.","An unlimited period applies only for fraud or when no return is filed.","A return filed before the due date is treated as filed on the due date. The three-year assessment period ends three years after April 15, 2024."],"wt":["calc","limit","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A return filed before the due date is treated as filed on the due date, so the 3-year assessment period runs from that date.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Early return is deemed filed on the due date, April 15, 2024","General period is 3 years","Ends April 15, 2027 (1,095 days after April 15, 2024)"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0005","t":"Marcus Ward's 2022 return reported gross income of $200,000. The IRS later finds that Marcus omitted $60,000 of gross income. There was no fraud. Under the general rules, how long does the IRS have to assess tax on the omitted income, measured from the filing of the return?","mc":{"s":"Marcus Ward's 2022 return reported gross income of $200,000. The IRS later finds that Marcus omitted $60,000 of gross income. There was no fraud. Under the general rules, how long does the IRS have to assess tax on the omitted income, measured from the filing of the return?","d":"","o":["6 years","3 years","No limit","2 years"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6501(e)(1)(A)","rat":"An omission of more than 25% of gross income shown on the return extends the period to 6 years.","ex":["An omission of more than 25% of gross income shown on the return extends the period to 6 years. 25% of $200,000 is $50,000, and $60,000 exceeds it.","This is the general period and ignores the substantial omission.","An unlimited period applies to fraud or a return that was not filed, neither of which applies.","Two years is part of the refund claim rule, not an assessment period."],"wt":["","calc","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Omitting more than 25% of gross income shown on the return extends the assessment period to 6 years.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gross income shown on the return: 200,000","25% threshold = 0.25 x 200,000 = 50,000","Omitted 60,000 > 50,000, so the period is 6 years"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0006","t":"The IRS mails a taxpayer a statutory notice of deficiency for 2024. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"The IRS mails a taxpayer a statutory notice of deficiency for 2024. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The taxpayer must pay the deficiency before a petition to the Tax Court is accepted","The taxpayer may petition the Tax Court within 90 days without paying first","The taxpayer has 30 days from the notice date to petition the Tax Court","The IRS may assess the deficiency immediately, before the 90 days end"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6212; IRC §6213(a)","rat":"The notice of deficiency gives 90 days (150 if addressed outside the United States) to petition the Tax Court, and the tax need not be paid first.","ex":["Prepayment is required in district court and the Court of Federal Claims, not in the Tax Court.","The notice of deficiency gives 90 days (150 if addressed outside the United States) to petition the Tax Court, and the tax need not be paid first.","Thirty days is the period of the 30-day letter, not the notice of deficiency.","Assessment is barred during the 90-day period and while a timely Tax Court case is pending."],"wt":["concept","","limit","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A notice of deficiency gives 90 days to petition the Tax Court without prepaying, and bars assessment meanwhile.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0007","t":"The IRS mailed a notice of deficiency on September 15, 2026, to Owen Grant, who lives in Texas. What is the last day to file a Tax Court petition?","mc":{"s":"The IRS mailed a notice of deficiency on September 15, 2026, to Owen Grant, who lives in Texas. What is the last day to file a Tax Court petition?","d":"","o":["October 15, 2026","January 13, 2027","December 14, 2026","February 12, 2027"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6213(a)","rat":"A U.S.","ex":["Uses 30 days, the 30-day letter period.","Uses 120 days, which is not a statutory period for this notice.","A U.S. resident has 90 days from the mailing date. 90 days after September 15, 2026 is December 14, 2026.","Uses 150 days, which applies only when the notice is addressed to a person outside the United States."],"wt":["limit","limit","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A U.S. resident has 90 days from the mailing of a notice of deficiency to petition the Tax Court.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Mailing date September 15, 2026","Add 90 days","December 14, 2026 (a Monday)"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0008","t":"Wesley Park wants a jury trial in his tax dispute over a $60,000 deficiency, and is willing to pay the tax first. Which forum should he choose?","mc":{"s":"Wesley Park wants a jury trial in his tax dispute over a $60,000 deficiency, and is willing to pay the tax first. Which forum should he choose?","d":"","o":["The Tax Court","The Court of Federal Claims","The IRS Independent Office of Appeals, through a formal conference","A U.S. district court, after paying the tax"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §7422; 28 U.S.C. §1346(a)(1)","rat":"Only the district court offers a jury trial, and it requires payment of the tax before a refund suit.","ex":["The Tax Court does not use juries.","This court does not use juries.","Appeals is an administrative process and has no juries.","Only the district court offers a jury trial, and it requires payment of the tax before a refund suit."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Only a U.S. district court offers a jury trial, and it requires payment of the tax before a refund suit.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0009","t":"Tessa Grant, an individual residing in Columbus, Ohio, loses a regular Tax Court case. To which court does she appeal?","mc":{"s":"Tessa Grant, an individual residing in Columbus, Ohio, loses a regular Tax Court case. To which court does she appeal?","d":"","o":["The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit","The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit","The U.S. Court of Appeals for the D.C. Circuit","The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7482(b)(1)","rat":"A Tax Court decision is reviewed by the court of appeals for the circuit of the taxpayer's residence; Ohio is in the Sixth Circuit.","ex":["A Tax Court decision is reviewed by the court of appeals for the circuit of the taxpayer's residence; Ohio is in the Sixth Circuit.","The Federal Circuit hears appeals from the Court of Federal Claims, not from the Tax Court.","The D.C. Circuit reviews a Tax Court case only if the taxpayer resides there or the parties agree in writing.","Illinois, Indiana and Wisconsin are in the Seventh Circuit, not Ohio."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A Tax Court decision is appealed to the court of appeals for the circuit of the taxpayer's residence.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0010","t":"The Tax Court entered its decision in a regular case on February 4, 2026. By what date must a party file a notice of appeal?","mc":{"s":"The Tax Court entered its decision in a regular case on February 4, 2026. By what date must a party file a notice of appeal?","d":"","o":["March 6, 2026","May 5, 2026","June 4, 2026","August 3, 2026"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7483","rat":"A notice of appeal must be filed within 90 days after the decision is entered.","ex":["Counts 30 days, which is the period for a motion to revise the decision.","A notice of appeal must be filed within 90 days after the decision is entered. February 4 plus 90 days is May 5, 2026.","Counts 120 days, which is longer than the statutory period.","Counts 180 days, which is longer than the statutory period."],"wt":["limit","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A notice of appeal from a Tax Court decision must be filed within 90 days after the decision is entered.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Decision entered February 4, 2026","Add 90 days","May 5, 2026 (a Tuesday)"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0011","t":"Ivan Rossi filed a timely Tax Court petition after receiving a notice of deficiency for 2023. Before the court decides, the IRS finds an additional understatement for the same year. What can the IRS do?","mc":{"s":"Ivan Rossi filed a timely Tax Court petition after receiving a notice of deficiency for 2023. Before the court decides, the IRS finds an additional understatement for the same year. What can the IRS do?","d":"","o":["Mail a second notice of deficiency for the same year at any time","Assess the additional amount immediately without a notice","Claim an increased deficiency in the pending Tax Court case","Nothing, since the IRS is bound by the amount in the original notice"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6212(c); IRC §6214(a)","rat":"After a petition is filed, the IRS generally cannot issue a second notice for the same year, but it may assert an increased deficiency in the pending case.","ex":["Once the petition is filed, a second notice is generally barred, except for fraud or similar exceptions.","Assessment of a deficiency requires the notice procedure, and the Tax Court case bars assessment.","After a petition is filed, the IRS generally cannot issue a second notice for the same year, but it may assert an increased deficiency in the pending case.","The court may redetermine a higher deficiency if the IRS asserts it."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"After a Tax Court petition is filed, the IRS generally cannot issue a second notice but may claim an increased deficiency in the pending case.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0012","t":"Marisol Vega receives a 30-day letter proposing a $60,000 deficiency. She disagrees and wants Appeals to consider the matter before any Tax Court case. What must she submit?","mc":{"s":"Marisol Vega receives a 30-day letter proposing a $60,000 deficiency. She disagrees and wants Appeals to consider the matter before any Tax Court case. What must she submit?","d":"","o":["A small case request","A Tax Court petition within 30 days","Nothing, because a 30-day letter automatically goes to Appeals","A formal written protest, since the amount exceeds $25,000"],"a":4,"cite":"Pub 5; IRM 8.6","rat":"For proposed amounts above $25,000, a formal written protest is required for an Appeals conference.","ex":["A small case request is used when the total amount for any tax period is $25,000 or less.","The Tax Court petition period runs from the notice of deficiency, not from the 30-day letter.","Appeals review requires the taxpayer to request it and, above $25,000, to file a protest.","For proposed amounts above $25,000, a formal written protest is required for an Appeals conference. A small case request is enough only at $25,000 or less."],"wt":["limit","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A proposed deficiency above $25,000 requires a formal written protest to obtain an Appeals conference.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1001","t":"Hannah Doyle timely filed her 2023 individual return, which reported gross income of $200,000. It left out $30,000 of income that she overlooked. The IRS has not begun an examination, and Hannah has not signed any agreement extending the assessment period. Which change in the facts would give the IRS an unlimited period to assess additional tax for 2023?","mc":{"s":"Hannah Doyle timely filed her 2023 individual return, which reported gross income of $200,000. It left out $30,000 of income that she overlooked. The IRS has not begun an examination, and Hannah has not signed any agreement extending the assessment period. Which change in the facts would give the IRS an unlimited period to assess additional tax for 2023?","d":"","o":["The omitted income turns out to be $60,000, which is more than 25% of the gross income stated on the return","Hannah left out the income on purpose, and the IRS proves she filed a false return intending to evade tax","Hannah filed the return on October 15, 2024 under a valid extension, rather than in March as she did","Hannah files an amended return that reports the omitted income and pays the additional tax with interest"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6501(a); IRC §6501(c)(1); IRC §6501(e)(1)(A)","rat":"A false or fraudulent return filed with intent to evade tax leaves the assessment period open indefinitely.","ex":["Omitting more than 25% of the gross income stated on the return extends the period to six years, not to an unlimited period.","A false or fraudulent return filed with intent to evade tax leaves the assessment period open indefinitely.","A return filed under extension is still subject to the three-year period, which then runs from the later filing date.","An amended return does not create an unlimited period. Reporting the income voluntarily does not extend the general three-year period."],"wt":["limit","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Unlimited assessment time applies to fraud or unfiled returns; omitting over 25% of gross income gives six years.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1003","t":"Renee Baptiste filed her 2023 individual return on February 12, 2024. The return was due April 15, 2024, and she did not extend it. All of her tax for 2023 was paid through withholding. In September 2026 she finds an overlooked deduction that would produce a refund. What is the last day on which she can file a timely claim for refund?","mc":{"s":"Renee Baptiste filed her 2023 individual return on February 12, 2024. The return was due April 15, 2024, and she did not extend it. All of her tax for 2023 was paid through withholding. In September 2026 she finds an overlooked deduction that would produce a refund. What is the last day on which she can file a timely claim for refund?","d":"","o":["April 15, 2026","February 12, 2027","April 15, 2027","October 15, 2027"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6511(a); IRC §6513(a); IRC §6513(b)(1)","rat":"The early return is treated as filed on April 15, 2024, and withholding is treated as paid the same day.","ex":["Counts two years from the day withholding is treated as paid. The claim period is the later of that date and three years from filing, so the two-year date is too early.","Counts three years from the actual early filing date. A return filed before its due date is treated as filed on the due date.","The early return is treated as filed on April 15, 2024, and withholding is treated as paid the same day. The later of three years from filing and two years from payment is April 15, 2027.","Adds a six-month extension to the three years. Renee did not extend the return, so no extra time applies."],"wt":["limit","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A refund claim is due by the later of three years from filing (early returns count as filed on the due date) or two years from payment.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["A return filed early is treated as filed on the due date, April 15, 2024","Withholding is treated as paid on April 15, 2024","Claim period is the later of 3 years from filing or 2 years from payment: 3 years from April 15, 2024","April 15, 2027 (1,095 days after April 15, 2024)"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1004","t":"The exhibit shows a notice of deficiency mailed to Kenji Arai. Kenji wants to petition the Tax Court. What is the last day on which a petition is timely?","mc":{"s":"The exhibit shows a notice of deficiency mailed to Kenji Arai. Kenji wants to petition the Tax Court. What is the last day on which a petition is timely?","d":"","o":["January 5, 2027","March 5, 2027","March 6, 2027","March 8, 2027"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6213(a); IRC §7503","rat":"A notice addressed to a person outside the United States allows 150 days.","ex":["Uses 90 days, which applies to a notice addressed to a person in the United States.","Moves back to the previous business day. A deadline that falls on a weekend moves forward to the next business day.","Correctly counts 150 days but ignores that March 6, 2027 is a Saturday.","A notice addressed to a person outside the United States allows 150 days. 150 days after October 7, 2026 is Saturday, March 6, 2027, so the deadline moves to Monday, March 8, 2027."],"wt":["limit","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A notice of deficiency mailed outside the U.S. allows 150 days, and a weekend deadline moves to the next business day.","tb":[["Item","Detail"],["Document","Statutory notice of deficiency"],["Taxpayer","Kenji Arai"],["Tax year","2024"],["Address on the notice","Osaka, Japan"],["Date the notice was mailed","Wednesday, October 7, 2026"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Notice is addressed to a person outside the United States, so the period is 150 days","October 7, 2026 + 150 days = March 6, 2027, a Saturday","A deadline on a Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday moves to the next business day: Monday, March 8, 2027"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1005","t":"The IRS mailed Elena Kowalczyk notices of deficiency for 2023 and 2024 showing the amounts in the exhibit. Elena disputes every amount. For which year or years may she ask the Tax Court to use its small tax case procedure?","mc":{"s":"The IRS mailed Elena Kowalczyk notices of deficiency for 2023 and 2024 showing the amounts in the exhibit. Elena disputes every amount. For which year or years may she ask the Tax Court to use its small tax case procedure?","d":"","o":["Both years, because each year's deficiency by itself is $50,000 or less","Neither year, because the two years together exceed $50,000","Neither year, because the small tax case procedure is not available when a penalty is asserted","2024 only, because the deficiency and penalty in dispute for that year total $50,000 or less"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §7463(a)","rat":"The limit applies to each tax year and counts penalties.","ex":["The amount in dispute includes penalties. Adding the $8,800 penalty puts 2023 above $50,000.","The limit is applied separately to each taxable year, not to the total of all years in the case.","Penalties may be part of a small tax case. They simply count toward the limit.","The limit applies to each tax year and counts penalties. 2024 totals $25,200, which qualifies, but 2023 totals $52,800, which does not."],"wt":["calc","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The small tax case limit is $50,000 per tax year including penalties, applied separately to each year.","tb":[["Tax year","Deficiency","Accuracy-related penalty","Total in dispute"],["2023","$44,000","$8,800","$52,800"],["2024","$21,000","$4,200","$25,200"]],"from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1006","t":"Paolo Ricci received a notice of deficiency for 2023 showing a $52,000 deficiency. He did not petition the Tax Court within the time allowed, and the IRS assessed the tax. Paolo now believes the IRS was wrong on the merits. Which route to judicial review is still open to him?","mc":{"s":"Paolo Ricci received a notice of deficiency for 2023 showing a $52,000 deficiency. He did not petition the Tax Court within the time allowed, and the IRS assessed the tax. Paolo now believes the IRS was wrong on the merits. Which route to judicial review is still open to him?","d":"","o":["Pay the tax, file a timely refund claim, and sue for a refund in a district court or the Court of Federal Claims if it is denied","File a late petition in the Tax Court, which the court may accept for up to one year after the tax is assessed","Ask the Independent Office of Appeals to reopen the year, with its decision then reviewable in the Tax Court on petition","Sue in a district court to stop collection without paying first, because he disputes the assessment on the merits"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6213(a); IRC §6511(a); IRC §7421(a); Case Flora v. United States, 362 U.S. 145 (1960)","rat":"After the Tax Court route is lost, the remaining route is to pay in full, file a timely refund claim, and sue for a refund in a district court or the Court of Federal Claims.","ex":["After the Tax Court route is lost, the remaining route is to pay in full, file a timely refund claim, and sue for a refund in a district court or the Court of Federal Claims.","No general one-year grace period exists for a Tax Court petition after a notice of deficiency.","Appeals has no procedure that revives the Tax Court's review of an assessed deficiency.","A suit to restrain assessment or collection is generally barred, and a refund suit requires full payment first."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"After missing the Tax Court deadline, review requires paying in full, filing a refund claim and suing in district court or the Court of Federal Claims.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1007","t":"After an examination, the IRS offers Ilse Brandt two ways to settle her 2023 tax year: signing Form 870, a waiver of restrictions on assessment, or entering into a closing agreement on Form 866. Which statement correctly compares the two?","mc":{"s":"After an examination, the IRS offers Ilse Brandt two ways to settle her 2023 tax year: signing Form 870, a waiver of restrictions on assessment, or entering into a closing agreement on Form 866. Which statement correctly compares the two?","d":"","o":["Both are closing agreements, so neither the IRS nor Ilse may reopen 2023 except for fraud, malfeasance or misrepresentation","Form 870 permits assessment now and does not bar a refund claim, while Form 866 is final except for fraud or misrepresentation","Form 870 is a final closing agreement, while Form 866 lets Ilse pay and claim a refund for 2023 later if she disagrees","Form 866 only waives the restrictions on assessment, and Form 870 also settles every later year that raises the same issue"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7121(b); Pub 556; Form 870 (Rev. 3-1992), instructions: General Information, Who Must Sign; IRM 8.6.4.6 (Forms 866 and 906 give closing-agreement finality under IRC 7121)","rat":"Form 870 is not a final closing agreement, so it does not stop Ilse from paying and claiming a refund later.","ex":["Only the closing agreement has that finality. Form 870 is a waiver of restrictions on assessment and is not a closing agreement.","Form 870 is not a final closing agreement, so it does not stop Ilse from paying and claiming a refund later. A closing agreement is final and conclusive except for fraud, malfeasance or misrepresentation of a material fact.","This reverses the two forms. The closing agreement, not the waiver, is the final one.","Neither form settles later years. The closing agreement is the final agreement for the matters it covers."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Form 870 waives restrictions on assessment and permits a refund claim, while a Form 866 closing agreement is final except for fraud or misrepresentation.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1013","t":"The IRS examined Ravi Kapoor's 2023 return and sent a 30-day letter proposing a $70,000 deficiency. Ravi did not respond and never filed a protest. The IRS then mailed a notice of deficiency, and Ravi filed a timely Tax Court petition. Which statement about Appeals is correct?","mc":{"s":"The IRS examined Ravi Kapoor's 2023 return and sent a 30-day letter proposing a $70,000 deficiency. Ravi did not respond and never filed a protest. The IRS then mailed a notice of deficiency, and Ravi filed a timely Tax Court petition. Which statement about Appeals is correct?","d":"","o":["Ravi lost any chance at Appeals review when he ignored the 30-day letter, so the case can go only to trial","Appeals may consider a case only until a notice of deficiency is issued, so the petition ended its role in the case","Ravi can reach Appeals only by first paying the $70,000 and then filing a claim for refund with the IRS","Because Appeals has not yet considered the case, the IRS will generally refer the docketed case to Appeals for settlement talks"],"a":4,"cite":"Rev. Proc. 2016-22, §§3.01, 3.03, 4.01 (2016-15 I.R.B. 577); IRC §6213(a)","rat":"Chief Counsel generally refers a docketed case to Appeals for settlement consideration unless Appeals itself issued the notice of deficiency, the taxpayer opts out, or an exception applies, such as a case designated for litigation.","ex":["Ignoring the 30-day letter does not end Appeals review. Docketed cases are handled differently.","Appeals also handles docketed cases and can settle them after a petition is filed.","Appeals consideration does not require payment. Prepayment is a condition of a refund suit, not of Appeals.","Chief Counsel generally refers a docketed case to Appeals for settlement consideration unless Appeals itself issued the notice of deficiency, the taxpayer opts out, or an exception applies, such as a case designated for litigation. Appeals neither considered this case nor issued the notice, so referral is the general rule."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A docketed Tax Court case is generally referred to Appeals for settlement even if the taxpayer never filed a protest.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"6":[{"i":"REG-I-C-0013","t":"Which statement about disclosure and the accuracy-related penalty for a substantial understatement of income tax is correct?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about disclosure and the accuracy-related penalty for a substantial understatement of income tax is correct?","d":"","o":["The understatement is reduced for a disclosed item with a reasonable basis, unless it involves a tax shelter","Disclosure removes the penalty for an item even if the position has no reasonable basis","Disclosure is needed only for positions that have substantial authority","Disclosure reduces the understatement for tax shelter items if the taxpayer is a corporation with audited financials"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6662(d)(2)(B); Reg §1.6662-4(f)","rat":"Adequate disclosure plus a reasonable basis reduces the understatement used in the substantial understatement test.","ex":["Adequate disclosure plus a reasonable basis reduces the understatement used in the substantial understatement test. That relief is not available for tax shelter items.","Disclosure helps only when the position has a reasonable basis; a frivolous or baseless position is not cured by disclosure.","Positions with substantial authority need no disclosure; disclosure matters for positions that lack it.","The disclosure reduction is not available for tax shelter items for any taxpayer."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Adequate disclosure of an item with a reasonable basis reduces the substantial understatement, except for tax shelter items.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0014","t":"Which of the following is NOT considered authority for purposes of determining substantial authority under the accuracy-related penalty rules?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following is NOT considered authority for purposes of determining substantial authority under the accuracy-related penalty rules?","d":"","o":["A private letter ruling issued to another taxpayer","A law review article or treatise conclusion","A revenue ruling","An IRS notice"],"a":2,"cite":"Reg §1.6662-4(d)(3)","rat":"Conclusions reached in treatises, legal periodicals and opinions of tax professionals are not authority, though the sources they cite may be.","ex":["Private letter rulings count as authority for substantial authority purposes, although they are not precedent.","Conclusions reached in treatises, legal periodicals and opinions of tax professionals are not authority, though the sources they cite may be.","Revenue rulings are authority for this purpose.","IRS notices are authority for this purpose."],"wt":["trap","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Treatises, legal periodicals and opinions of tax professionals are not authority for substantial authority; rulings, notices and PLRs are.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0015","t":"Assume that each of the following return positions has a reasonable basis and none is a tax shelter or reportable transaction. Which position must be disclosed on the return for the taxpayer to avoid the accuracy-related penalty on it?","mc":{"s":"Assume that each of the following return positions has a reasonable basis and none is a tax shelter or reportable transaction. Which position must be disclosed on the return for the taxpayer to avoid the accuracy-related penalty on it?","d":"","o":["A position supported by a regular Tax Court decision on nearly identical facts","A position supported by a revenue ruling that addresses the same facts","A position that is contrary to a Treasury regulation, taken in good faith","A position supported by the plain language of the Internal Revenue Code"],"a":3,"cite":"Reg §1.6662-3(c); IRC §6662(d)(2)(B)","rat":"A position contrary to a regulation must be disclosed, on Form 8275-R, to avoid the penalty; substantial authority does not remove the disclosure requirement for it.","ex":["This is substantial authority, so disclosure is not needed to avoid the penalty.","A revenue ruling on point is substantial authority, so no disclosure is needed.","A position contrary to a regulation must be disclosed, on Form 8275-R, to avoid the penalty; substantial authority does not remove the disclosure requirement for it.","A position supported by statute has substantial authority, so no disclosure is needed."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A position contrary to a Treasury regulation must be disclosed on Form 8275-R even if held in good faith; positions with substantial authority need no disclosure.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0016","t":"Which of the following business expenses is subject to the strict substantiation requirements for travel, gifts and listed property?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following business expenses is subject to the strict substantiation requirements for travel, gifts and listed property?","d":"","o":["Office supplies bought for the business","Rent on the business's storefront","Advertising in a local newspaper","Lodging on an overnight business trip"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §274(d); Reg §1.274-5T; Pub 463","rat":"Travel away from home is subject to strict substantiation of the amount, time, place and business purpose.","ex":["Ordinary business expenses like supplies need reasonable substantiation, not the strict rules.","Rent is an ordinary expense subject to the general recordkeeping rule.","Advertising is an ordinary expense subject to the general recordkeeping rule.","Travel away from home is subject to strict substantiation of the amount, time, place and business purpose."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Travel, gifts and listed property expenses need strict substantiation of amount, time, place and business purpose; ordinary expenses need only reasonable records.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0017","t":"Lia Fontaine, a consultant, kept a contemporaneous log for a 2026 business trip recording the amount, date, place and purpose of each expense. She lost all receipts. Which expense lacks adequate substantiation under the travel rules?","mc":{"s":"Lia Fontaine, a consultant, kept a contemporaneous log for a 2026 business trip recording the amount, date, place and purpose of each expense. She lost all receipts. Which expense lacks adequate substantiation under the travel rules?","d":"","o":["A $68 hotel night recorded in the log","A $45 taxi ride recorded in the log","A $30 meal recorded in the log","A $62 parking fee recorded in the log"],"a":1,"cite":"Reg §1.274-5(c)(2)(iii); Pub 463","rat":"Documentary evidence such as a receipt is required for lodging regardless of the amount.","ex":["Documentary evidence such as a receipt is required for lodging regardless of the amount. The $75 exception does not apply to lodging.","For expenses under $75, a receipt is not required if the log has the required details.","For an expense under $75 the log is enough.","For an expense under $75 the log is enough."],"wt":["","trap","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Lodging always needs a receipt; other travel expenses under $75 need only a log with amount, time, place and purpose.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0018","t":"Rita Osei claims a deduction for worthless securities on her 2025 return. For how many years should she keep the supporting records, measured from the date she files, at a minimum?","mc":{"s":"Rita Osei claims a deduction for worthless securities on her 2025 return. For how many years should she keep the supporting records, measured from the date she files, at a minimum?","d":"","o":["3 years","4 years","6 years","7 years"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6001; Pub 583; IRC §6511(d)(1)","rat":"Records supporting a worthless securities or bad debt deduction should be kept 7 years.","ex":["Three years is the general period and does not cover this claim.","Four years is the period for employment tax records.","Six years applies where more than 25% of gross income is omitted.","Records supporting a worthless securities or bad debt deduction should be kept 7 years."],"wt":["limit","limit","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Records supporting a worthless securities or bad debt deduction should be kept 7 years.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0019","t":"Iris Wen, a U.S. citizen, holds two foreign bank accounts. The largest balance during 2025 was $6,000 in one account and $5,500 in the other, at different times of the year. She has no other foreign assets. Must she file an FBAR for 2025?","mc":{"s":"Iris Wen, a U.S. citizen, holds two foreign bank accounts. The largest balance during 2025 was $6,000 in one account and $5,500 in the other, at different times of the year. She has no other foreign assets. Must she file an FBAR for 2025?","d":"","o":["No, because neither account exceeded $10,000 on its own","Yes, because the maximum values add up to $11,500","No, because the balances were not above $10,000 on the same day","No, because an FBAR is required only for accounts above $100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"31 CFR §1010.350; 31 USC §5314; 31 CFR §1010.306(c)","rat":"An FBAR is required when the aggregate of the maximum values of all foreign financial accounts exceeded $10,000 at any time during the year, even if no single account did.","ex":["The test aggregates the accounts.","An FBAR is required when the aggregate of the maximum values of all foreign financial accounts exceeded $10,000 at any time during the year, even if no single account did.","The maximum values are added even if they occurred on different dates.","The threshold is $10,000 in aggregate."],"wt":["concept","","concept","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An FBAR is required when the aggregate maximum value of all foreign financial accounts exceeds $10,000 at any time in the year.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Maximum value account 1: 6,000","Maximum value account 2: 5,500","Aggregate 6,000 + 5,500 = 11,500 > 10,000, so an FBAR is required"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1002","t":"Ruth Alvarez, a self-employed consultant, kept no receipts, logs or other records of her 2025 business expenses. On examining her 2025 return, the IRS disallows all of her business deductions for lack of documentation. For which expense could a court still allow a reasonable estimate, if Ruth shows she incurred the expense and gives a reasonable basis for the amount?","mc":{"s":"Ruth Alvarez, a self-employed consultant, kept no receipts, logs or other records of her 2025 business expenses. On examining her 2025 return, the IRS disallows all of her business deductions for lack of documentation. For which expense could a court still allow a reasonable estimate, if Ruth shows she incurred the expense and gives a reasonable basis for the amount?","d":"","o":["Airfare and hotel for a client meeting in another city","The business share of her car's use, for which she kept no log","Office supplies bought for use in the consulting business","A holiday gift she gave to an important client"],"a":3,"cite":"Case Cohan v. Commissioner, 39 F.2d 540 (2d Cir. 1930); IRC §274(d); Pub 463; Reg. Treas. Reg. §1.274-5T(c)","rat":"Expenses outside the strict rules for travel, gifts and listed property may be estimated under the Cohan rule when the taxpayer shows the expense was incurred and gives a reasonable basis for the amount.","ex":["Travel expenses must meet the strict substantiation rules, so a court may not estimate them.","Listed property such as a vehicle is subject to the strict substantiation rules, and an estimate is not enough.","Expenses outside the strict rules for travel, gifts and listed property may be estimated under the Cohan rule when the taxpayer shows the expense was incurred and gives a reasonable basis for the amount. (Lost records from a casualty such as a flood could instead be reasonably reconstructed under Treas. Reg. §1.274-5T(c)(5); here no records were ever kept.)","Gifts are subject to the strict substantiation rules, so the court may not estimate the amount."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Cohan estimates are allowed for expenses outside the strict substantiation rules for travel, gifts and listed property.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"7":[{"i":"REG-I-C-0020","t":"Which statement about the civil fraud penalty on an underpayment is correct?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about the civil fraud penalty on an underpayment is correct?","d":"","o":["It is 20% of the underpayment, and the taxpayer must disprove fraud","It is 75% of the underpayment, and the IRS need only show fraud by a preponderance of the evidence","It is 75% of the fraudulent portion, and the IRS must prove fraud by clear and convincing evidence","It is 40% of the underpayment, and it applies with the accuracy-related penalty on the same portion"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6663(a); IRC §6663(b); IRC §7454(a)","rat":"The fraud penalty is 75% of the underpayment attributable to fraud, and the IRS bears the burden of proving fraud by clear and convincing evidence.","ex":["20% is the accuracy-related rate, and the IRS carries the burden on fraud.","The standard is clear and convincing evidence, which is higher than a preponderance.","The fraud penalty is 75% of the underpayment attributable to fraud, and the IRS bears the burden of proving fraud by clear and convincing evidence.","The fraud penalty is not 40%, and it is not stacked with the accuracy-related penalty on the same portion."],"wt":["limit","limit","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The civil fraud penalty is 75% of the fraud-attributable underpayment, and the IRS must prove fraud by clear and convincing evidence.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0021","t":"Which statement describes the failure-to-file addition to tax for a late return with tax due, when no fraud is involved?","mc":{"s":"Which statement describes the failure-to-file addition to tax for a late return with tax due, when no fraud is involved?","d":"","o":["0.5% of the unpaid tax for each month or part of a month, up to 25%, with no reduction for other penalties","5% of the unpaid tax for each month, up to 50%","15% of the unpaid tax for each month, up to 75%","5% per month or part of a month, up to 25%, reduced by the failure-to-pay amount"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6651(a)(1); IRC §6651(c)(1)","rat":"The failure-to-file addition is 5% per month or fraction of a month, capped at 25%, and reduced by the failure-to-pay addition for the same months.","ex":["0.5% is the failure-to-pay rate, not the failure-to-file rate.","The cap for the failure-to-file addition is 25%, not 50%.","This is the rate and cap when the failure to file is fraudulent.","The failure-to-file addition is 5% per month or fraction of a month, capped at 25%, and reduced by the failure-to-pay addition for the same months."],"wt":["limit","limit","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The failure-to-file addition is 5% per month or part of a month up to 25%, reduced by the failure-to-pay addition.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0022","t":"Bianca Rossi's 2025 return was due April 15, 2026. She did not extend it and filed on June 29, 2026, paying the $10,000 balance due with the return. She has no reasonable cause. Ignoring interest and the minimum penalty, what are the total failure-to-file and failure-to-pay additions?","mc":{"s":"Bianca Rossi's 2025 return was due April 15, 2026. She did not extend it and filed on June 29, 2026, paying the $10,000 balance due with the return. She has no reasonable cause. Ignoring interest and the minimum penalty, what are the total failure-to-file and failure-to-pay additions?","d":"","o":["$150","$1,350","$1,500","$1,650"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6651(a)(1); IRC §6651(a)(2); IRC §6651(c)(1)","rat":"The return was 3 months late (2 months and 14 days counts as 3).","ex":["Counts only the failure-to-pay addition and ignores the failure-to-file addition.","Counts only the failure-to-file addition net of the overlap and omits the failure-to-pay addition.","The return was 3 months late (2 months and 14 days counts as 3). The failure-to-file rate is 5% less 0.5% when both apply, so 4.5% x 3 = 13.5%, plus 0.5% x 3 = 1.5% failure to pay. Total 15% of $10,000.","Adds the full 5% failure-to-file rate and the 0.5% failure-to-pay rate without the reduction for months when both apply."],"wt":["calc","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For overlapping months, the failure-to-file rate is 4.5% plus 0.5% failure-to-pay per month or part of a month.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Months late: April 15 to June 29 is 2 months and 14 days, counted as 3 months","Failure to file net of overlap: 4.5% x 3 = 13.5%","Failure to pay: 0.5% x 3 = 1.5%","15% x 10,000 = 1,500"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0023","t":"Hollis Grant, the owner of a small company, gave his 2025 return to his CPA in March 2026 and asked that it be filed on time. The CPA overlooked the deadline and filed on May 12, 2026, with tax due. The IRS asserts the failure-to-file addition. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Hollis Grant, the owner of a small company, gave his 2025 return to his CPA in March 2026 and asked that it be filed on time. The CPA overlooked the deadline and filed on May 12, 2026, with tax due. The IRS asserts the failure-to-file addition. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Hollis has no reasonable cause, because the filing deadline is a nondelegable duty","Hollis has reasonable cause, because he relied on a CPA","The penalty is waived automatically because it is Hollis's first late filing and the CPA caused the delay","The penalty applies only to the CPA and not to Hollis"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6651(a)(1); Case United States v. Boyle (1985)","rat":"Under the Supreme Court's Boyle decision, reliance on an agent to file on time is not reasonable cause, since the deadline is the taxpayer's own nondelegable duty.","ex":["Under the Supreme Court's Boyle decision, reliance on an agent to file on time is not reasonable cause, since the deadline is the taxpayer's own nondelegable duty.","Reliance on a professional excuses substantive tax advice errors, not missing a filing deadline.","First-time abatement is an administrative relief the taxpayer must request and qualify for; it is not automatic.","The failure-to-file addition is assessed on the taxpayer."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Relying on an agent to file on time is not reasonable cause for late filing; the deadline is the taxpayer's nondelegable duty (Boyle).","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0024","t":"Simone Alves's return, filed on time, shows tax of $80,000. The correct tax is $110,000. The entire $30,000 understatement results from a failure to make a reasonable attempt to keep adequate records, and there is no fraud or reasonable cause. What is the accuracy-related penalty attributable to negligence?","mc":{"s":"Simone Alves's return, filed on time, shows tax of $80,000. The correct tax is $110,000. The entire $30,000 understatement results from a failure to make a reasonable attempt to keep adequate records, and there is no fraud or reasonable cause. What is the accuracy-related penalty attributable to negligence?","d":"","o":["$3,000","$6,000","$12,000","$22,500"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6662(a); IRC §6662(c); Reg §1.6662-3","rat":"Negligence draws an accuracy-related penalty of 20% of the portion of the underpayment attributable to it: 20% x $30,000 = $6,000.","ex":["Uses 10%, which is the substantial understatement threshold percentage, not the penalty rate.","Negligence draws an accuracy-related penalty of 20% of the portion of the underpayment attributable to it: 20% x $30,000 = $6,000.","Uses 40%, which applies only to gross valuation misstatements and undisclosed noneconomic substance transactions.","Uses the 75% civil fraud rate, and no fraud is asserted."],"wt":["limit","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The accuracy-related penalty for negligence is 20% of the underpayment attributable to it.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Negligence: accuracy-related penalty is 20% of the attributable underpayment","20% x 30,000 = 6,000"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0025","t":"Priya Raman's individual return showed tax of $64,000. The IRS later determines the correct tax is $73,000. There was no substantial authority for the position, no disclosure, no reasonable cause and no fraud. What accuracy-related penalty applies for a substantial understatement?","mc":{"s":"Priya Raman's individual return showed tax of $64,000. The IRS later determines the correct tax is $73,000. There was no substantial authority for the position, no disclosure, no reasonable cause and no fraud. What accuracy-related penalty applies for a substantial understatement?","d":"","o":["$0","$1,800","$3,600","$6,750"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6662(a); IRC §6662(b)(2); IRC §6662(d)(1)(A)","rat":"The understatement of $9,000 exceeds the greater of 10% of the correct tax ($7,300) or $5,000, so it is substantial.","ex":["Uses a $10,000 minimum, which applies to corporations, so the understatement wrongly looks below the threshold.","The understatement of $9,000 exceeds the greater of 10% of the correct tax ($7,300) or $5,000, so it is substantial. The penalty is 20% x $9,000 = $1,800.","Applies 40%, which is not the rate for a substantial understatement.","Applies the 75% civil fraud rate. Fraud is not asserted."],"wt":["limit","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An understatement is substantial if above the greater of 10% of correct tax or $5,000 for individuals; the accuracy penalty is 20%.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Understatement = 73,000 - 64,000 = 9,000","Threshold = greater of 10% x 73,000 = 7,300 or 5,000 = 7,300","9,000 > 7,300, so substantial","20% x 9,000 = 1,800"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0026","t":"Malcolm and Dawn Reyes filed a joint return. The IRS proves that Malcolm's fraud caused an underpayment of $40,000. Dawn did not know of or take part in the fraud. Which statement about the civil fraud penalty is correct?","mc":{"s":"Malcolm and Dawn Reyes filed a joint return. The IRS proves that Malcolm's fraud caused an underpayment of $40,000. Dawn did not know of or take part in the fraud. Which statement about the civil fraud penalty is correct?","d":"","o":["It applies to both spouses because they filed a joint return","The fraud penalty applies to Malcolm on the portion due to his fraud, but not to Dawn","It applies to neither spouse, because joint returns are exempt from the fraud penalty","It applies only to Dawn because she signed the return with him"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6663(c)","rat":"On a joint return, the fraud penalty applies to a spouse only if some part of the underpayment is due to that spouse's fraud.","ex":["The penalty follows each spouse's own fraud.","On a joint return, the fraud penalty applies to a spouse only if some part of the underpayment is due to that spouse's fraud.","There is no exemption for joint returns.","Signing a joint return does not make her the fraudulent party."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"On a joint return the civil fraud penalty applies only to the spouse whose fraud caused the underpayment.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0027","t":"Marlow Corp. engages in a transaction that lacks economic substance. It does not disclose the transaction on its return. The underpayment attributable to the transaction is $100,000, and no exception applies. What accuracy-related penalty applies?","mc":{"s":"Marlow Corp. engages in a transaction that lacks economic substance. It does not disclose the transaction on its return. The underpayment attributable to the transaction is $100,000, and no exception applies. What accuracy-related penalty applies?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$25,000","$40,000","$75,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6662(b)(6); IRC §6662(i)","rat":"The penalty for an undisclosed noneconomic substance transaction is 40% of the underpayment: 40% x $100,000 = $40,000.","ex":["Uses 20%, the rate when the transaction is adequately disclosed.","Uses 25%, which is the maximum failure-to-file cap and is unrelated to this penalty.","The penalty for an undisclosed noneconomic substance transaction is 40% of the underpayment: 40% x $100,000 = $40,000.","Uses the 75% civil fraud rate. Fraud is not asserted."],"wt":["limit","limit","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The accuracy-related penalty for an undisclosed transaction lacking economic substance is 40% of the underpayment.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Undisclosed noneconomic substance: 40% of the underpayment","40% x 100,000 = 40,000"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0028","t":"Emil Sato sold property in 2025 and reported an adjusted basis of $500,000. The correct adjusted basis was $100,000. The overstated basis caused an underpayment of $60,000. There was no fraud or reasonable cause. What accuracy-related penalty applies?","mc":{"s":"Emil Sato sold property in 2025 and reported an adjusted basis of $500,000. The correct adjusted basis was $100,000. The overstated basis caused an underpayment of $60,000. There was no fraud or reasonable cause. What accuracy-related penalty applies?","d":"","o":["$12,000","$24,000","$45,000","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6662(e); IRC §6662(h)","rat":"A reported basis that is 200% or more of the correct amount is a gross valuation misstatement (and the $60,000 underpayment exceeds the $5,000 minimum), so the penalty is 40%: 40% x $60,000 = $24,000.","ex":["Uses 20%, which applies to a substantial valuation misstatement (basis at least 150% but less than 200% of the correct amount).","A reported basis that is 200% or more of the correct amount is a gross valuation misstatement (and the $60,000 underpayment exceeds the $5,000 minimum), so the penalty is 40%: 40% x $60,000 = $24,000.","Uses the 75% civil fraud rate, which is not asserted.","Treats the whole underpayment as the penalty."],"wt":["limit","","limit","calc"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A reported basis of 200% or more of the correct amount is a gross valuation misstatement with a 40% penalty.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Reported basis / correct basis = 500,000 / 100,000 = 500% (>= 200%)","Gross valuation misstatement: 40%","40% x 60,000 = 24,000"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0029","t":"Carla Nunez filed her 2025 return 20 days late. She has had no penalties in the prior three years, filed all required returns, and paid the tax due. She asks the IRS to waive the failure-to-file addition. Which administrative relief is most likely available?","mc":{"s":"Carla Nunez filed her 2025 return 20 days late. She has had no penalties in the prior three years, filed all required returns, and paid the tax due. She asks the IRS to waive the failure-to-file addition. Which administrative relief is most likely available?","d":"","o":["Automatic waiver, because the return was less than 30 days late","No relief is available for a late return, because the penalty is mandatory","First-time abatement, based on her clean three-year history","Relief only through a Tax Court petition"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6651(a); IRM 20.1.1.3.3.2.1","rat":"The IRS may waive certain penalties administratively if the taxpayer had no penalties in the prior three years, filed all required returns, and paid or arranged to pay the tax.","ex":["There is no automatic waiver based on lateness.","Reasonable cause and first-time abatement are available.","The IRS may waive certain penalties administratively if the taxpayer had no penalties in the prior three years, filed all required returns, and paid or arranged to pay the tax.","Administrative relief may be requested from the IRS without a court case."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"First-time penalty abatement is available administratively for taxpayers with a clean three-year history who filed and paid.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0030","t":"Gavin Pryce gave his CPA all the facts about a complex transaction. The CPA, who was experienced in that area, advised a return position. Gavin relied in good faith, but the IRS later disallowed the position. What is the effect on an accuracy-related penalty?","mc":{"s":"Gavin Pryce gave his CPA all the facts about a complex transaction. The CPA, who was experienced in that area, advised a return position. Gavin relied in good faith, but the IRS later disallowed the position. What is the effect on an accuracy-related penalty?","d":"","o":["Gavin has no defense, because a disallowed position carries the penalty","Gavin has a defense only if the CPA is also an attorney","Gavin has a defense only if he verified the CPA's conclusions with a second, independent adviser before filing","Gavin may avoid the penalty; good-faith reliance on a competent, fully informed adviser is reasonable cause"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §6664(c); Reg §1.6664-4(c); Case Neonatology Associates v. Commissioner","rat":"Reasonable cause can be shown when the taxpayer gave the adviser complete information, the adviser was competent, and the taxpayer relied in good faith.","ex":["Reasonable cause and good faith is a defense to the penalty.","The adviser need not be an attorney.","A second opinion is not required.","Reasonable cause can be shown when the taxpayer gave the adviser complete information, the adviser was competent, and the taxpayer relied in good faith."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Good-faith reliance on a competent, fully informed adviser can establish reasonable cause and avoid the accuracy-related penalty.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1008","t":"Alberto Rojas's return understated his tax. The exhibit summarizes the revenue agent's findings. What total civil penalty applies to the underpayment?","mc":{"s":"Alberto Rojas's return understated his tax. The exhibit summarizes the revenue agent's findings. What total civil penalty applies to the underpayment?","d":"","o":["$24,000","$60,000","$68,000","$90,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6663(a); IRC §6663(b); IRC §6662(a); IRC §6662(b)","rat":"The fraud penalty of 75% applies to the $80,000 fraud portion ($60,000).","ex":["20% of the whole $120,000. This ignores the fraud penalty that applies to the portion due to fraud.","75% of the $80,000 fraud portion only. It omits the 20% accuracy-related penalty on the remaining $40,000.","The fraud penalty of 75% applies to the $80,000 fraud portion ($60,000). Because Alberto proved the other $40,000 is not fraud, the 20% accuracy-related penalty applies to it ($8,000). Total $68,000.","75% of the entire $120,000. That result applies only if Alberto fails to prove that the $40,000 is not due to fraud."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The fraud penalty is 75% of the fraud portion, and the 20% accuracy penalty applies to the remaining portion shown not fraudulent.","tb":[["Revenue agent's summary","Amount"],["Total underpayment","$120,000"],["Portion the IRS proves is due to fraud","$80,000"],["Portion Alberto proves, by a preponderance of the evidence, is not due to fraud (substantial understatement; no defense applies)","$40,000"]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Fraud penalty: 75% x 80,000 = 60,000","Alberto proves 40,000 is not fraud, so the 20% accuracy-related penalty applies to it: 20% x 40,000 = 8,000","Total = 60,000 + 8,000 = 68,000"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1009","t":"The exhibit summarizes an examination of Sophie Lindqvist's individual return. What accuracy-related penalty applies to the $90,000 underpayment?","mc":{"s":"The exhibit summarizes an examination of Sophie Lindqvist's individual return. What accuracy-related penalty applies to the $90,000 underpayment?","d":"","o":["$14,000","$18,000","$28,000","$52,500"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6662(a); IRC §6662(b)(1); IRC §6662(b)(2); IRC §6662(d)(2)(B); Reg §1.6662-2(c)","rat":"Item 2 is disclosed with a reasonable basis, so no penalty applies to it.","ex":["Item 2 is disclosed with a reasonable basis, so no penalty applies to it. Item 1 is both negligent and a substantial understatement, but penalties do not stack: 20% x $70,000 = $14,000.","20% of the full $90,000. It ignores that Item 2 is disclosed with a reasonable basis and is not penalized.","40% of $70,000. It adds the negligence and substantial-understatement rates together, but no stacking of rates is allowed.","75% of $70,000, the civil fraud rate. Fraud is not asserted."],"wt":["","calc","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Accuracy-related penalties do not stack, and a disclosed item with reasonable basis is not penalized.","tb":[["Item","Underpayment","Facts","Disclosed on Form 8275?"],["1","$70,000","No reasonable attempt to keep adequate records; no substantial authority; no reasonable cause","No"],["2","$20,000","Position with a reasonable basis; not a tax shelter","Yes"],["Total","$90,000","Tax shown $60,000; correct tax $150,000; no fraud",""]],"from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Item 2 ($20,000) is adequately disclosed and has a reasonable basis: no penalty","Item 1 understatement after reduction: 90,000 - 20,000 = 70,000; threshold = greater of 10% x 150,000 = 15,000 or 5,000 = 15,000; 70,000 > 15,000, so substantial","Negligence and substantial understatement overlap on the same 70,000; the rate does not stack","20% x 70,000 = 14,000"]}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1010","t":"The IRS disallowed a deduction on each of four unrelated returns and asserted the accuracy-related penalty. Each taxpayer claims reasonable cause based on reliance on someone else. Which taxpayer is most likely to establish reasonable cause and good faith?","mc":{"s":"The IRS disallowed a deduction on each of four unrelated returns and asserted the accuracy-related penalty. Each taxpayer claims reasonable cause based on reliance on someone else. Which taxpayer is most likely to establish reasonable cause and good faith?","d":"","o":["Vera, who bought an aggressive shelter on the advice of the promoter who sold it and who earned a fee from her investment","Dev, who hired an experienced CPA but described the deduction only as a consulting expense and left out that the payee was his own company","Gwen, who gave an experienced CPA all the relevant facts and took the position the CPA recommended after reviewing the authorities","Owen, who followed the tax advice of his brother-in-law, a bookkeeper with no tax training, because it saved the most tax"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6664(c)(1); Reg §1.6664-4(c); Case Neonatology Associates, P.A. v. Commissioner, 115 T.C. 43 (2000), aff'd, 299 F.3d 221 (3d Cir. 2002)","rat":"Reasonable cause is shown when the adviser is competent, the taxpayer gives the adviser complete and accurate information, and the taxpayer actually relies in good faith.","ex":["Reliance on a promoter who profits from the transaction is generally not reasonable.","Reliance fails when the taxpayer withholds facts the adviser needs, so the advice was not based on all the relevant facts.","Reasonable cause is shown when the adviser is competent, the taxpayer gives the adviser complete and accurate information, and the taxpayer actually relies in good faith. Gwen meets all three.","The adviser must be competent in tax matters. Reliance on someone without that expertise is not reasonable."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Reasonable cause requires a competent adviser, complete information and good-faith reliance.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1011","t":"The 2025 returns of the four taxpayers in the exhibit were due April 15, 2026. None has reasonable cause for any failure. Which taxpayer owes the failure-to-file addition to tax?","mc":{"s":"The 2025 returns of the four taxpayers in the exhibit were due April 15, 2026. None has reasonable cause for any failure. Which taxpayer owes the failure-to-file addition to tax?","d":"","o":["Nia","Petra","Omar","Quinn"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6651(a)(1); IRC §6651(b)(1); IRC §6081(a)","rat":"She had no extension and filed late with $3,000 of tax unpaid at the due date, so the failure-to-file addition applies to that unpaid tax.","ex":["Her return showed a refund, so no tax was unpaid on the due date. The addition is figured on the unpaid tax, so it is zero.","She had no extension and filed late with $3,000 of tax unpaid at the due date, so the failure-to-file addition applies to that unpaid tax.","His extension was timely and he filed within the extended due date, so he owes no failure-to-file addition. He may owe a failure-to-pay addition.","He filed late, but his payments covered all of his tax, so no tax was unpaid and the addition is zero."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The failure-to-file addition applies to tax unpaid at the original due date when the return is filed late without a valid extension.","tb":[["Taxpayer","Extension requested?","Date 2025 return filed","Result shown on the return"],["Nia","No","July 20, 2026","Refund; withholding exceeded her tax"],["Petra","No","July 20, 2026","$3,000 balance due, paid with the return"],["Omar","Yes, by April 15, 2026","October 10, 2026","$6,000 balance due, paid with the return"],["Quinn","No","August 3, 2026","No balance; estimated payments and withholding covered all of his tax"]],"from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-1012","t":"Gordon Pell believes that wages are not income. He filed a signed 2024 return reporting zero income, and the return itself showed that its self-assessed tax was wrong. After the IRS issued a notice of deficiency, Gordon petitioned the Tax Court and repeated the same argument, which courts have rejected many times. Which penalty aimed at frivolous positions may apply to each action?","mc":{"s":"Gordon Pell believes that wages are not income. He filed a signed 2024 return reporting zero income, and the return itself showed that its self-assessed tax was wrong. After the IRS issued a notice of deficiency, Gordon petitioned the Tax Court and repeated the same argument, which courts have rejected many times. Which penalty aimed at frivolous positions may apply to each action?","d":"","o":["$5,000 for the return and up to $25,000 for the petition","$25,000 for the return and $5,000 for the petition","Nothing for the return, because a return reporting no income is not a return, and up to $25,000 for the petition","$5,000 for the return and nothing for the petition, because a Tax Court petition cannot be penalized"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §6702(a); IRC §6673(a)(1)","rat":"The IRS may assess a $5,000 penalty for a frivolous return, and the Tax Court may award up to $25,000 against a taxpayer whose position in the case is frivolous or groundless.","ex":["The IRS may assess a $5,000 penalty for a frivolous return, and the Tax Court may award up to $25,000 against a taxpayer whose position in the case is frivolous or groundless.","The amounts are reversed. $5,000 applies to the frivolous return and up to $25,000 to a frivolous Tax Court position.","The frivolous return penalty reaches a document that purports to be a return and shows on its face that the tax is wrong, so it applies to the return.","The Tax Court may award damages of up to $25,000 when a taxpayer's position is frivolous or groundless."],"wt":["","limit","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The IRS may assess a $5,000 penalty on a frivolous return, and the Tax Court may award up to $25,000 for frivolous positions.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"8":[{"i":"REG-I-C-0031","t":"Which of the following carries the highest level of authority for federal tax purposes?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following carries the highest level of authority for federal tax purposes?","d":"","o":["The Internal Revenue Code","A Treasury final regulation","A revenue ruling","A Tax Court memorandum decision"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7805(a); Reg §1.6662-4(d)(3)","rat":"The Code is statute enacted by Congress and outranks regulations, rulings and IRS guidance.","ex":["The Code is statute enacted by Congress and outranks regulations, rulings and IRS guidance.","Regulations interpret the Code and are subordinate to it.","Revenue rulings are IRS interpretations and rank below regulations.","A memorandum decision applies to the case decided and is weaker than statute."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The Internal Revenue Code, as enacted statute, outranks regulations, rulings and court decisions.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-C-0032","t":"Which type of IRS guidance may be relied on as precedent only by the taxpayer to whom it was issued?","mc":{"s":"Which type of IRS guidance may be relied on as precedent only by the taxpayer to whom it was issued?","d":"","o":["A revenue ruling","A private letter ruling","A revenue procedure","A final regulation"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §6110(k)(3)","rat":"A private letter ruling may not be used or cited as precedent by other taxpayers.","ex":["Taxpayers with substantially similar facts may rely on a revenue ruling.","A private letter ruling may not be used or cited as precedent by other taxpayers.","A revenue procedure states procedural rules that apply to all taxpayers.","A final regulation binds all taxpayers."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A private letter ruling may not be cited as precedent by taxpayers other than the one to whom it was issued.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"9":[{"i":"REG-I-D-0001","t":"In which of the following proceedings does the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege protect a client's tax-advice communications with a CPA?","mc":{"s":"In which of the following proceedings does the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege protect a client's tax-advice communications with a CPA?","d":"","o":["A civil examination of the client's return by the IRS","A criminal tax prosecution of the client","A state department of revenue audit of the same return","An SEC investigation of the client's financial statements"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7525(a)","rat":"The privilege applies to tax advice communications in noncriminal tax matters before the IRS and in federal court proceedings brought by or against the United States.","ex":["The privilege applies to tax advice communications in noncriminal tax matters before the IRS and in federal court proceedings brought by or against the United States.","The privilege does not apply in criminal matters.","The privilege applies to federal tax matters, not to state proceedings.","The privilege is limited to noncriminal federal tax matters; it does not reach an SEC investigation."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The federal tax practitioner privilege covers tax advice only in noncriminal federal tax matters, not criminal, state or SEC proceedings.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0002","t":"The IRS summons the workpapers a CPA prepared while completing a client's Form 1040. The workpapers contain the information the client gave the CPA for use in preparing the return. Is the information privileged under the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege?","mc":{"s":"The IRS summons the workpapers a CPA prepared while completing a client's Form 1040. The workpapers contain the information the client gave the CPA for use in preparing the return. Is the information privileged under the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege?","d":"","o":["Yes, because all communications with a CPA about taxes are privileged","No, because return-preparation information is not tax advice","Yes, if the CPA is also an enrolled agent","Yes, but only if the client signs a written confidentiality agreement"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7525(a); Case United States v. Frederick (7th Cir. 1999)","rat":"The privilege covers tax advice.","ex":["The privilege is limited to tax advice, not to every communication.","The privilege covers tax advice. Information intended for the return, and the return preparation itself, is not protected.","The extra credential does not change the nature of the communication.","The privilege does not depend on a signed agreement."],"wt":["trap","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The tax practitioner privilege protects tax advice, not information given for return preparation or the preparation itself.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0003","t":"A client with potential criminal exposure wants to discuss a return position with an accountant so that the discussion is least likely to be reachable by an IRS summons. Which arrangement is most likely to protect the communications?","mc":{"s":"A client with potential criminal exposure wants to discuss a return position with an accountant so that the discussion is least likely to be reachable by an IRS summons. Which arrangement is most likely to protect the communications?","d":"","o":["The client hires the accountant directly and the accountant prepares workpapers for the client","The accountant signs a confidentiality agreement with the client","The client's attorney engages the accountant to help the attorney give legal advice","The client gives the records to the accountant and the accountant asserts the client's Fifth Amendment rights"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §7525; Case United States v. Kovel; Case Couch v. United States","rat":"Communications with an accountant retained by the attorney to assist with legal advice can fall under the attorney-client privilege.","ex":["The tax practitioner privilege does not cover criminal matters, and a direct engagement gains no attorney-client protection.","A private agreement cannot create a privilege against an IRS summons.","Communications with an accountant retained by the attorney to assist with legal advice can fall under the attorney-client privilege.","The Fifth Amendment does not protect records in the accountant's possession."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Communications with an accountant engaged by the client's attorney to help give legal advice can fall under attorney-client privilege, including in criminal matters.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0004","t":"Karen Doyle gave her accountant her business records for preparing her return. The IRS issues a summons to the accountant for those records. Can Karen block production on Fifth Amendment grounds?","mc":{"s":"Karen Doyle gave her accountant her business records for preparing her return. The IRS issues a summons to the accountant for those records. Can Karen block production on Fifth Amendment grounds?","d":"","o":["Yes, because the records still belong to her","Yes, but only if she notifies the IRS within 30 days","Yes, because a federal accountant-client privilege protects records given to a CPA for return preparation","No, because the Fifth Amendment does not reach records held by her accountant"],"a":4,"cite":"Case Couch v. United States (1973)","rat":"The Supreme Court held that a taxpayer has no Fifth Amendment claim when the IRS summons her accountant's possession of her records.","ex":["Ownership does not create Fifth Amendment protection when someone else holds them and is summoned.","A notice period does not create a constitutional claim.","There is no general common-law accountant-client privilege in federal tax matters.","The Supreme Court held that a taxpayer has no Fifth Amendment claim when the IRS summons her accountant's possession of her records."],"wt":["trap","limit","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The Fifth Amendment does not protect a taxpayer's records held by her accountant when the IRS summons them.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0005","t":"The IRS summons an independent auditor's tax accrual workpapers related to a corporation's financial statements. What is the general rule on a claim that the workpapers are privileged?","mc":{"s":"The IRS summons an independent auditor's tax accrual workpapers related to a corporation's financial statements. What is the general rule on a claim that the workpapers are privileged?","d":"","o":["Courts recognize no accountant work-product privilege here, so the IRS may obtain them","They are protected by a federal accountant-client privilege","They are protected by the attorney work-product doctrine because the auditor works for the company","They are protected until the company gives written consent"],"a":1,"cite":"Case United States v. Arthur Young & Co. (1984)","rat":"The Supreme Court declined to create a work-product privilege for independent auditors' tax accrual workpapers.","ex":["The Supreme Court declined to create a work-product privilege for independent auditors' tax accrual workpapers.","No general federal accountant-client privilege exists.","An independent auditor is not the company's attorney, so attorney work product does not apply.","Consent is irrelevant to a valid summons."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"No federal accountant work-product privilege protects an independent auditor's tax accrual workpapers from an IRS summons.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0006","t":"A state statute gives communications between an accountant and a client a privilege from disclosure. Does that privilege protect the communications from an IRS summons in a federal tax matter?","mc":{"s":"A state statute gives communications between an accountant and a client a privilege from disclosure. Does that privilege protect the communications from an IRS summons in a federal tax matter?","d":"","o":["Yes, because federal courts follow the privilege rules of the state where the accountant is licensed","No, because federal privilege law governs and no federal accountant-client privilege exists","Yes, but only for documents dated after the statute took effect","Yes, unless the IRS agrees to waive the privilege"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7525; Case Couch v. United States (1973)","rat":"State-law privileges do not bind federal tax proceedings; only the limited federal tax practitioner privilege applies.","ex":["In federal question cases, federal privilege law governs.","State-law privileges do not bind federal tax proceedings; only the limited federal tax practitioner privilege applies.","Dates are irrelevant because state privilege does not bind a federal summons.","The IRS cannot waive a state privilege that does not apply."],"wt":["concept","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Federal privilege law governs federal tax matters, so a state accountant-client privilege does not bar an IRS summons.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0007","t":"Bruno Fields, CPA, misses the deadline for an election, and his client loses a $40,000 tax benefit. Which basic legal theory best describes the client's claim against Bruno?","mc":{"s":"Bruno Fields, CPA, misses the deadline for an election, and his client loses a $40,000 tax benefit. Which basic legal theory best describes the client's claim against Bruno?","d":"","o":["Fraud, because a missed deadline that costs the client money is presumed to be deceptive","Strict liability, because the loss is certain","Negligence, because Bruno failed to use ordinary professional care","Criminal conspiracy, because the client lost money"],"a":3,"cite":"Case Restatement (Second) of Torts §299A; AICPA Code 1.300.001","rat":"Professionals must perform with reasonable care and competence, and a client can recover damages from a breach that causes a loss.","ex":["Fraud requires an intentional misstatement, which is not present.","Professional services do not carry strict liability.","Professionals must perform with reasonable care and competence, and a client can recover damages from a breach that causes a loss.","A missed deadline is not a crime."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A professional who fails to use ordinary care and causes client loss is liable in negligence, not fraud or strict liability.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0008","t":"Halcyon CPA prepares a written tax opinion for a promoter, knowing the promoter will give it to a small, identified group of investors who will rely on it. The opinion is negligently wrong and the investors are harmed. Under the Restatement (Second) of Torts approach to third-party liability, what is the result?","mc":{"s":"Halcyon CPA prepares a written tax opinion for a promoter, knowing the promoter will give it to a small, identified group of investors who will rely on it. The opinion is negligently wrong and the investors are harmed. Under the Restatement (Second) of Torts approach to third-party liability, what is the result?","d":"","o":["Halcyon cannot be liable, because only the promoter, its client, has a contract with it","Halcyon is liable to every person who could foreseeably read the opinion","Halcyon is liable only if it committed fraud","Halcyon may be liable to the known group of investors it expected to rely"],"a":4,"cite":"Case Restatement (Second) of Torts §552","rat":"Under the Restatement approach, a professional may be liable for negligence to a known limited class of persons for whose benefit and guidance the information is supplied.","ex":["This states a strict privity rule that the Restatement approach rejects for known limited groups.","The Restatement limits liability to a known class, not to all foreseeable readers.","Negligence is sufficient for a known limited group.","Under the Restatement approach, a professional may be liable for negligence to a known limited class of persons for whose benefit and guidance the information is supplied."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the Restatement (Second) of Torts, a professional may be liable for negligence to a known limited class relying on the work.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0009","t":"Which situation most clearly supports a conviction for tax evasion, as opposed to only a misdemeanor for willful failure to file or pay?","mc":{"s":"Which situation most clearly supports a conviction for tax evasion, as opposed to only a misdemeanor for willful failure to file or pay?","d":"","o":["A taxpayer who knows he owes tax and hides cash in nominee accounts to keep the IRS from collecting it","A taxpayer who knows the balance due on a timely filed return and simply does not pay it","A taxpayer who misses the filing deadline because of illness and files two months late","A taxpayer who keeps poor records because of carelessness"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7201; IRC §7203; Case Sansone v. United States","rat":"Evasion requires a tax deficiency, an affirmative act, and willfulness.","ex":["Evasion requires a tax deficiency, an affirmative act, and willfulness. Hiding assets in nominee accounts is a classic affirmative act.","Willful failure to pay, without an affirmative act to evade, is a misdemeanor.","Illness suggests lack of willfulness, so neither offense applies.","Carelessness is negligence and is not criminal evasion."],"wt":["","concept","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax evasion requires a deficiency, an affirmative act and willfulness; merely not paying is a misdemeanor.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0010","t":"Generally, what is the statute of limitations for the government to bring criminal charges for tax evasion or for filing a false return?","mc":{"s":"Generally, what is the statute of limitations for the government to bring criminal charges for tax evasion or for filing a false return?","d":"","o":["3 years","5 years","6 years","10 years"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §6531","rat":"Most criminal tax offenses, including evasion and false returns, must be charged within 6 years.","ex":["Three years is the general civil assessment period, not the criminal period.","Five years is not the criminal limit for these offenses.","Most criminal tax offenses, including evasion and false returns, must be charged within 6 years.","Ten years is longer than the criminal limit."],"wt":["limit","limit","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Most criminal tax offenses, including evasion and false returns, must be charged within 6 years.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0011","t":"Grady Simms was charged with willfully failing to file returns. He testified that he sincerely believed his wages were not income under the tax law. The belief was unreasonable. Under Supreme Court precedent, what is the effect of the belief on willfulness?","mc":{"s":"Grady Simms was charged with willfully failing to file returns. He testified that he sincerely believed his wages were not income under the tax law. The belief was unreasonable. Under Supreme Court precedent, what is the effect of the belief on willfulness?","d":"","o":["The belief is irrelevant, because ignorance of the law is not a defense","A sincere belief can negate willfulness even if it is unreasonable","The belief negates willfulness only if it was reasonable","The belief establishes willfulness, because taxpayers must know the tax law"],"a":2,"cite":"Case Cheek v. United States (1991); IRC §7203","rat":"Willfulness requires knowledge of a legal duty.","ex":["For willful tax offenses, knowledge of the legal duty is an element, so good-faith misunderstanding matters.","Willfulness requires knowledge of a legal duty. A genuine good-faith misunderstanding of the law can negate it even if it is objectively unreasonable.","The Court held that unreasonableness does not by itself defeat good faith.","Knowledge of the law is a factual question, not conclusively presumed."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Willfulness requires knowledge of the legal duty, so a sincere good-faith belief can negate it even if unreasonable.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0012","t":"Which remedy may a federal court order against a return preparer who repeatedly prepares returns with understatements caused by unreasonable or willful positions?","mc":{"s":"Which remedy may a federal court order against a return preparer who repeatedly prepares returns with understatements caused by unreasonable or willful positions?","d":"","o":["An order that the preparer serve mandatory community service with the IRS","A Tax Court order revoking the preparer's state CPA license","An injunction that bars the person from acting as a tax return preparer","Automatic imprisonment for 3 years without a criminal trial"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §7407","rat":"The government may seek an injunction against a preparer who engages in specified conduct, including continual or flagrant misconduct, that bars the person from preparing returns.","ex":["No provision authorizes this remedy.","The Tax Court has no authority over state licensing.","The government may seek an injunction against a preparer who engages in specified conduct, including continual or flagrant misconduct, that bars the person from preparing returns.","Prison requires a criminal conviction."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A court may enjoin a preparer who continually or flagrantly engages in specified misconduct from preparing returns.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0013","t":"Which of the following communications is NOT protected by the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege, even if it is otherwise tax advice in a civil federal tax matter?","mc":{"s":"Which of the following communications is NOT protected by the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege, even if it is otherwise tax advice in a civil federal tax matter?","d":"","o":["Advice on how to respond to an IRS notice of deficiency","Advice about the tax consequences of a proposed merger","Advice on how to structure a like-kind exchange","A written communication promoting a tax shelter"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §7525(b)","rat":"The privilege does not extend to written communications in connection with promoting direct or indirect participation in a tax shelter.","ex":["This is tax advice in a civil matter, and is covered.","This is tax advice and is covered in a civil federal tax matter.","This is tax advice and is covered in a civil federal tax matter.","The privilege does not extend to written communications in connection with promoting direct or indirect participation in a tax shelter."],"wt":["trap","trap","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The tax practitioner privilege does not cover written communications promoting participation in a tax shelter.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-0014","t":"After the IRS summons a client's records, the client tells CPA Lena Ortiz to shred her workpapers so the IRS cannot get them. What is the consequence if Lena does so?","mc":{"s":"After the IRS summons a client's records, the client tells CPA Lena Ortiz to shred her workpapers so the IRS cannot get them. What is the consequence if Lena does so?","d":"","o":["It may be felony obstruction and may lead to Circular 230 sanctions","None, because the workpapers belong to Lena and she may dispose of them","A civil accuracy-related penalty of 20% on the client","Only an ethics violation under the AICPA Code, with no criminal exposure"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7212(a); C230 §10.51(a)","rat":"Destroying records that are summoned can be obstruction under the tax code and is conduct for which a practitioner can be disciplined.","ex":["Destroying records that are summoned can be obstruction under the tax code and is conduct for which a practitioner can be disciplined.","Destroying records to defeat a summons is not a permitted use of one's own property.","That penalty applies to underpayments, not to destroying records.","Criminal exposure can apply to obstruction."],"wt":["","trap","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Destroying summoned records can be obstruction and grounds for Circular 230 discipline.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1001","t":"Foster & Quill, CPAs, audited Tarn Holdings' financial statements. The firm knew that Tarn would give the audited statements to one or more banks to obtain a loan of about $2,000,000, but it did not know which banks and had no contact with any bank. The audit was negligent. Lakeview Bank lent Tarn $2,000,000 relying on the statements, and Ines Ward, who bought Tarn stock on the open market, also relied on them. Both lost money. Under which approach to accountants' liability to third parties could Lakeview recover for ordinary negligence, but Ines could not?","mc":{"s":"Foster & Quill, CPAs, audited Tarn Holdings' financial statements. The firm knew that Tarn would give the audited statements to one or more banks to obtain a loan of about $2,000,000, but it did not know which banks and had no contact with any bank. The audit was negligent. Lakeview Bank lent Tarn $2,000,000 relying on the statements, and Ines Ward, who bought Tarn stock on the open market, also relied on them. Both lost money. Under which approach to accountants' liability to third parties could Lakeview recover for ordinary negligence, but Ines could not?","d":"","o":["The Ultramares approach, which requires privity of contract with the auditor","The Credit Alliance approach, which requires a known user and conduct linking it to the auditor","The Restatement (Second) of Torts approach, which covers a limited group of intended users","The foreseeability approach, which covers users whose reliance the auditor could reasonably foresee"],"a":3,"cite":"Case Ultramares Corp. v. Touche, 255 N.Y. 170 (1931); Case Credit Alliance Corp. v. Arthur Andersen & Co., 65 N.Y.2d 536 (1985); Restatement (Second) of Torts §552; Case Rosenblum v. Adler, 93 N.J. 324 (1983)","rat":"Under Restatement Section 552 the auditor is liable to a limited group it knows will use the statements for a particular transaction.","ex":["Neither Lakeview nor Ines had a contract with the auditor, so neither could recover for ordinary negligence.","The auditor did not know which banks would receive the statements and had no contact with any, so neither plaintiff meets the test.","Under Restatement Section 552 the auditor is liable to a limited group it knows will use the statements for a particular transaction. The banks Tarn would approach for the loan are such a group, but open-market stock buyers are not.","This approach reaches every foreseeable user, so Ines could recover along with Lakeview."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under Restatement (Second) of Torts section 552, an auditor is liable to a known limited group of intended users, not open-market buyers.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1002","t":"A state follows the strict privity approach of Ultramares. Mira Voss, CPA, issued an unmodified opinion on Corvane Inc.'s financial statements without confirming any accounts receivable or inspecting inventory, even though her own workpapers flagged large unexplained sales. The statements later proved materially false. Corvane's lender, which had no contract with Mira and was unknown to her, sued for its loss. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"A state follows the strict privity approach of Ultramares. Mira Voss, CPA, issued an unmodified opinion on Corvane Inc.'s financial statements without confirming any accounts receivable or inspecting inventory, even though her own workpapers flagged large unexplained sales. The statements later proved materially false. Corvane's lender, which had no contract with Mira and was unknown to her, sued for its loss. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The lender cannot recover on any theory, because it had no contract with Mira and she did not know it","The lender may recover if it proves that Mira was grossly negligent or reckless, which counts as constructive fraud","The lender may recover only if Mira knew who the lender was when she issued the unmodified opinion","The lender may recover on a showing of ordinary negligence, because its loss from the false statements was foreseeable"],"a":2,"cite":"Case Ultramares Corp. v. Touche, 255 N.Y. 170 (1931)","rat":"Under Ultramares, gross negligence so serious that it implies fraud can support liability to a third party who relied, even without privity.","ex":["Privity is not required when the auditor's conduct is fraudulent, and reckless disregard of obvious warning signs can be treated as constructive fraud.","Under Ultramares, gross negligence so serious that it implies fraud can support liability to a third party who relied, even without privity.","Knowing the identity of the user matters for negligence claims under other approaches, not for liability based on fraud or constructive fraud.","Foreseeability supports ordinary negligence liability only in states that follow the foreseeability approach. This state follows strict privity."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under Ultramares, a third party may recover without privity if the auditor's gross negligence or recklessness amounts to constructive fraud.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1003","t":"Two investors sue an auditor over the same materially misstated financial statements. Ada bought shares in a registered public offering and sues under the Securities Act of 1933 provision that makes experts liable for misstatements in a registration statement. Ben bought shares on the open market and sues under the general antifraud provisions of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934. Which of the following must Ben prove that Ada need not prove?","mc":{"s":"Two investors sue an auditor over the same materially misstated financial statements. Ada bought shares in a registered public offering and sues under the Securities Act of 1933 provision that makes experts liable for misstatements in a registration statement. Ben bought shares on the open market and sues under the general antifraud provisions of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934. Which of the following must Ben prove that Ada need not prove?","d":"","o":["That the auditor acted with intent to deceive or reckless disregard and that Ben relied on the report","That the audited financial statements contained a material misstatement or omission when the shares were sold","That Ben suffered a monetary loss on the shares he bought after the statements were issued","That the auditor had a contract with Ben or knew who he was when it issued the audit report"],"a":1,"cite":"Securities Act §11 (15 U.S.C. §77k); Exchange Act §10(b) and Rule 10b-5; Case Ernst & Ernst v. Hochfelder, 425 U.S. 185 (1976)","rat":"The antifraud claim requires scienter and reliance.","ex":["The antifraud claim requires scienter and reliance. The registration statement claim generally requires neither.","Ada must also prove a material misstatement in the registration statement, so this is not an added burden for Ben.","Both plaintiffs must show damages, so this is not an added burden for Ben.","Neither claim requires privity of contract or knowledge of the plaintiff's identity."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A 1934 Act antifraud claim requires scienter and reliance, unlike a 1933 Act registration statement claim.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1004","t":"Purchasers in a public offering sue the company's auditors under the Securities Act of 1933, alleging a material misstatement in the audited financial statements included in the registration statement. Which of the following is a valid defense for the auditors?","mc":{"s":"Purchasers in a public offering sue the company's auditors under the Securities Act of 1933, alleging a material misstatement in the audited financial statements included in the registration statement. Which of the following is a valid defense for the auditors?","d":"","o":["The purchasers did not read or rely on the audited statements when they bought their shares in the offering","The auditors had no contract with the purchasers and had no contact with them before the offering","The auditors did not intend to deceive anyone and believed in good faith that the statements were fairly presented","The auditors made a reasonable investigation and reasonably believed the statements were true when the registration became effective"],"a":4,"cite":"Securities Act §11(b)(3)(B) (15 U.S.C. §77k(b)(3)(B))","rat":"This is the due diligence defense for experts.","ex":["Reliance is generally not an element of the registration statement claim, so lack of reliance is not a defense.","Privity is not required for the registration statement claim.","Intent is not an element, and a good-faith belief without a reasonable investigation does not meet the due diligence defense.","This is the due diligence defense for experts. The auditors must show a reasonable investigation and a reasonable ground to believe, and actual belief, that the statements were true."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An expert's due diligence defense under the 1933 Act requires a reasonable investigation and reasonable belief that the statements were true.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1005","t":"Delphine Ross, CPA, gave her client Arclight Corp. a memorandum of tax advice about a proposed reorganization. The memorandum is protected by the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege in a civil tax matter. Which later event most likely waives the privilege for the memorandum?","mc":{"s":"Delphine Ross, CPA, gave her client Arclight Corp. a memorandum of tax advice about a proposed reorganization. The memorandum is protected by the federally authorized tax practitioner privilege in a civil tax matter. Which later event most likely waives the privilege for the memorandum?","d":"","o":["Arclight's chief financial officer, who needs the advice to carry out the plan, shares the memorandum with two finance managers","Arclight gives a copy of the memorandum to its outside lender to help win approval of a related loan","Delphine discusses the memorandum with a partner in her firm who is helping on the engagement","The IRS opens an examination of Arclight's return and asks about the reorganization"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §7525(a)","rat":"Voluntary disclosure of privileged advice to an outsider such as a lender waives the privilege for that communication.","ex":["Sharing within the client on a need-to-know basis does not waive the privilege.","Voluntary disclosure of privileged advice to an outsider such as a lender waives the privilege for that communication.","Consulting within the practitioner's own firm on the engagement does not waive the privilege.","An IRS examination or request for information does not waive the privilege. Only the client's voluntary disclosure does."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Voluntary disclosure of privileged tax advice to an outsider such as a lender waives the tax practitioner privilege.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1006","t":"Reese Duran, a paid return preparer, knowingly listed fictitious business expenses on a client's return to increase the client's refund. The client, Alma, believed the return was accurate, did not know about the expenses, and signed it. Which statement about Reese's criminal exposure is correct?","mc":{"s":"Reese Duran, a paid return preparer, knowingly listed fictitious business expenses on a client's return to increase the client's refund. The client, Alma, believed the return was accurate, did not know about the expenses, and signed it. Which statement about Reese's criminal exposure is correct?","d":"","o":["He may be convicted of willfully assisting in a false return, even though Alma did not know it was false","He cannot be convicted, because Alma signed the return herself and had no intent to defraud anyone","He may be convicted only if the IRS also proves that Alma is liable for the civil fraud penalty on the return","He faces at most a misdemeanor, because preparers are not subject to the felony provisions of the tax code"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §7206(2)","rat":"The statute reaches anyone who willfully aids or assists in preparing a false return, whether or not the taxpayer knows of the falsity.","ex":["The statute reaches anyone who willfully aids or assists in preparing a false return, whether or not the taxpayer knows of the falsity.","The taxpayer's signature and innocence do not protect a preparer who willfully assists in a false return.","No finding of civil fraud against the client is required for the preparer's conviction.","Willfully assisting in a false return is a felony that applies to preparers."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A preparer who willfully assists in preparing a false return commits a felony even if the taxpayer is unaware of the falsity.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"REG-I-D-1007","t":"In a private class action under the antifraud provisions of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, a jury finds that Larkin & Shaw, the company's auditor, is 30% responsible for the investors' loss because it acted recklessly but not knowingly. The company's officers, who acted knowingly, are found 70% responsible. How is Larkin & Shaw's liability for damages determined?","mc":{"s":"In a private class action under the antifraud provisions of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, a jury finds that Larkin & Shaw, the company's auditor, is 30% responsible for the investors' loss because it acted recklessly but not knowingly. The company's officers, who acted knowingly, are found 70% responsible. How is Larkin & Shaw's liability for damages determined?","d":"","o":["Jointly and severally liable for all of the damages, because any auditor found liable under the antifraud provisions is jointly and severally liable","Not liable at all, because an auditor is liable under the antifraud provisions only if it knew the statements were false","Generally liable only for its own percentage of responsibility, because reckless conduct is not treated as a knowing violation","Jointly and severally liable, because the case was certified as a class action"],"a":3,"cite":"Exchange Act §21D(f)(2)(A)-(B), (f)(10)(B) (15 U.S.C. §78u-4(f)(2)(A)-(B), (f)(10)(B)); Statute Private Securities Litigation Reform Act of 1995","rat":"Under the Private Securities Litigation Reform Act, joint and several liability requires a knowing violation.","ex":["Joint and several liability applies only to a defendant that knowingly committed the violation, not to every auditor found liable.","Recklessness satisfies the scienter requirement in most courts, so actual knowledge is not needed for liability.","Under the Private Securities Litigation Reform Act, joint and several liability requires a knowing violation. Reckless conduct is not treated as knowing, so liability is generally proportionate.","Class certification does not change the liability rule."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"I","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the PSLRA, joint and several liability requires a knowing violation, so reckless defendants are liable only proportionately.","from":"2026-01-01"}}]};
window.PAIRS=[];
